WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.396 --> 00:00:03.680
Reiner Pope is the co-founder
and CEO of MatX.

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:06.200
He's a former math whiz and Haskell
programmer who became

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.680
a TPU architect for Google.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:10.960
Now he's teamed up with Google's former
chief chip architect to design

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.137
a better chip for AI.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:20.320
A year ago, everyone was saying
Google is canceled.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:22.720
AI is going to eat their search.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.640
No one's going to search for things,
and therefore, the business won't do well.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.160
Obviously, that sentiment has really
shifted, in part helped by

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.080
Gemini 3 is really good.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:34.240
Then also it's really fast.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.800
It's powered by the custom
chip hardware Google has.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.124
You were inside Google for a lot
of the foundational period,

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.000
laying the groundwork for that stuff.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:48.400
What do people not
appreciate about what Google did right

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:52.040
to lay all the groundwork
for their current AI success?

00:00:52.552 --> 00:00:53.880
They started with the research.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:55.800
The transformers came from there.

00:00:55.847 --> 00:00:59.880
Pretty much anyone who's
over 30 and at a large lab has

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.580
been at Google Brain at some point.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.941
I think there was and has
been a lot of talent there.

00:01:06.441 --> 00:01:09.240
TPUs are pretty good.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.600
We think there's better you can do,
of course, but they at least had

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.920
the option, the opportunity to design
the TPUs for neural nets at least, rather

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.040
than graphics applications like NVIDIA.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.000
The overall architecture,
starting with single core doing

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.720
what was at the time reasonably large
systolic arrays, by today's standards,

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.594
nowhere near as much, but I think those
were a lot of really good decisions.

00:01:35.095 --> 00:01:36.760
When did the TPU project start?

00:01:37.097 --> 00:01:40.840
TPU v1 was announced in 2016, I think.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:46.200
That was what led to the creation
of all of those 2016–2017 startups.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.609
Cerebras, Groq, Graphcore,
SambaNova, all of those.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:53.120
TPU v1 is a really impressive project.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.560
It was done on a very short timeline.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.600
I don't know the full details,
but maybe about a year or so,

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:03.040
maybe a year and a half,
with a skeleton team of 20-30 people.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:06.126
Really, really minimal viable product.

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:09.360
More recent TPUs and more recent AI chips
in general

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:15.880
can't do that because the market has
moved, and the table

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:16.800
stakes are much higher.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:19.840
The first-generation product, they just…

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:23.640
One big systolic array,
stick a memory next to it, we're done.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.647
It was really simple,
nice, elegant product.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:29.960
Obviously, TPU v1
predates the Transformer.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:34.560
Is that just a coincidence that they
happened at very similar times, or

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:39.600
are they related in some way?
There was a period of maybe about four

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:46.920
years of a lot of ML research, or neural

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:48.880
net research, prior to Transformer.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:50.160
What was popular?

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.716
LSTMs and ConvNets
and ResNet and Inception.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:00.880
The big thinking at the time was
to adapt it to be used for LSTMs.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:04.351
It's a reasonable fit there.

00:03:04.434 --> 00:03:07.520
But I think there was just
a huge flurry of activity.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:10.440
Why did it all happen then and not later?

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:13.193
It's probably just because
people stopped publishing.

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:17.800
2022 was about the time when Google
completely stopped

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:18.840
publishing its research.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.300
All the good papers are
from before that as a result.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.720
But is there some hand-wavy story you can
tell about parallelization where both

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:29.800
Transformers and TPUs are

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:33.960
about really internalizing

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:35.640
the importance of parallelization?

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.480
Definitely,
I put it somewhat on people, actually.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:45.120
It is just true.
Hardware is massively parallel.

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:49.520
You've got tens of billions,
hundreds of billions of transistors

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:53.760
on your chip, and it takes
maybe 100 clock cycles to get from one

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:55.120
side of the chip to the other.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.200
You can't do a sequential computation
involving transistors

00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:59.080
on both sides of the chip.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:01.860
The hardware is just fundamentally
parallel, and you have to

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:03.080
take advantage of that.

00:04:03.118 --> 00:04:06.360
TPU v1 and all later TPUs
naturally took advantage of that.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.760
Matrix Multiply is really nice
because it is so parallel.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:14.760
I think on the hardware side
that's generally understood.

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:18.920
I think most ML researchers,
especially of the time,

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:25.080
were not super deep in what
hardware wants, and what is…

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:28.060
Mechanical sympathy is sometimes
a term that's used for that.

00:04:29.728 --> 00:04:32.360
What is the term?
It kind of— It speaks for itself.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.320
Think about the poor machine
and what does it want?

00:04:37.736 --> 00:04:39.060
What does it want?

00:04:39.196 --> 00:04:44.320
The term actually, I think,
originates in maybe high-frequency trading

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.120
in areas like that, which is,
I haven't worked in.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:48.920
I've just,
I like reading about the software

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:50.420
that people have built from there.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:52.600
It's like, for them,
what does the machine want?

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:54.360
It wants a lot
of instruction-level parallelism.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.004
This is CPUs, not TPUs.

00:04:56.087 --> 00:05:02.880
Once a lot don't branch, so unpredictable

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:04.120
branches kill your performance.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.240
Think about the things that CPUs
do and how to use them best.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.000
Can I get to peak performance on a CPU?

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:10.060
It's that idea.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.320
I think the whole idea of peak
performance on a CPU is kind of crazy.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:17.000
No one even says,
"What is peak performance?

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:21.000
What is my percentage of peak
on a CPU?" Because the performance

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:25.160
of software running on CPUs is really bad,
but running on GPUs, or TPUs,

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:27.640
or AI chips in general,
actually that is the main focus.

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:28.960
It's like, "What is my percentage of peak?

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:32.920
Can I get 70% or 80%?"
I feel like many people listening to this

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:38.200
know that GPUs perform better
for AI workloads than CPUs.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:41.720
It's a funny history when you think about
it, where just

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:47.640
one day we woke up with all these very
mathematically intensive workloads,

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:52.920
first crypto mining, and then AI,
so then NVIDIA is extremely

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:56.640
well-positioned because they've been
making GPUs for gamers

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:57.760
that you would plug into...

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:01.903
You'd buy your
Dell PC back in the day and maybe upgrade

00:06:02.404 --> 00:06:07.080
the graphics card by plugging in a better
NVIDIA graphics card than the one

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:09.440
the stock Dell computer came with.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:12.960
They were incredibly
well-positioned to capture that.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:14.240
I think people know that.

00:06:14.249 --> 00:06:19.640
What is the intuitive explanation
as to why GPUs are better

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:21.280
for AI workloads than CPUs?

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:26.160
People say, "They're better for these
mathematical computations." But that's

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:28.840
a tautological answer, basically.

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:31.680
Is there some way you can have a mental
model for why that is the case?

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:35.562
Because software instruction
sets also involve doing math.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:39.960
Intuitions, I'm not sure.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:44.880
Let me try and just go to
some of the big differences, which is,

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:50.280
really wide vector instructions is
the hallmark of a GPU,

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:54.372
which I think it's maybe…

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:58.720
If you want some intuition, it's like how
much is spent on controlling the thing?

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:01.960
Maybe control means,
if I'm driving a truck, how much…

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:03.720
Is the driver versus the payload?

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:06.280
A truck has a huge payload in it.

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:09.840
That's more like the GPU, whereas
maybe a motorcycle is more

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:11.500
like the CPU, where you've got…

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:15.800
Actually just processing the instructions,
reading, "What do I have to do next?

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:19.760
How do I do that?"
That is most of the cost on a CPU, whereas

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:24.920
if you just keep the same instructions
but make the payload 100 times bigger,

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:29.280
then you can shift most of the cost to be
in the actual work that you want to do.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:35.920
CPUs have been optimized for very complex
instruction sets, whereas

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:40.200
GPU is optimized for—
Complex instruction sets and

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:44.860
fine-grained changing what you want to do.

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:49.480
Like steering, in this analogy,
a CPU can steer an obstacle course,

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:52.920
no problem, whereas,
on a GPU, you're just going to go

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:54.808
straight line for a really long time.

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:56.860
This is getting us into...

00:07:58.120 --> 00:07:59.320
What is MatX?

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:01.800
How did you guys start it,
and which part of this

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:02.760
space are you attacking?

00:08:02.816 --> 00:08:08.520
MatX is making the best chips
physically possible for LLMs.

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:16.040
What led us into MatX…

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:17.800
Mike is the other founder.

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:19.720
Mike and I were both working at Google.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:28.120
I was working on the inference stack
for running LLMs, and I was saying,

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:34.400
"How can we make the best software on TPUs
for running LLMs?" Then what we really

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:37.040
wanted out of hardware was support much,
much larger matrices.

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:41.240
The matrices have grown from maybe 128
in dimension into the many thousands.

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:45.960
Truck goes to many trailers.

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:53.000
Much larger matrices and
much lower precision arithmetic.

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:57.880
We tried to move the TPUs
in this direction.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:00.800
TPUs have been moving in this direction,
but they're constrained

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:03.200
by a lot of other workloads.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:05.040
There was a big ads workload at the time.

00:09:06.838 --> 00:09:12.800
Back in '22, before ChatGPT was released,
there was this idea that LLMs were going

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:18.400
to be a big thing, but not conviction,
and really hard to make a big bet on that.

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:22.560
I think a startup is more of the right
place to make a big bet on a workload.

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:24.355
If you fail, it's fine.

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:26.800
Another startup will succeed.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:31.960
Whereas I think a company like Google or
NVIDIA, the next chip

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:33.300
has to work for sure.

00:09:34.365 --> 00:09:37.440
You can take more technical
risks as they turn up.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:40.800
Actually, I would say we were taking
product risks rather than technical risks.

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:42.800
But is there actually product risk?

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:45.293
Because it seems like
LLMs are going to work.

00:09:45.877 --> 00:09:47.440
I think now we understand it.

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:51.000
Two or three years ago,
I think it was just like a— And when

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.400
you say the best chips for LLMs...

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:54.640
I can think of multiple
ways to measure best.

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:56.200
It could be the best performance per watt.

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:59.000
It could be the lowest latency,
capable of handling the largest models.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:09:59.641
What is best?

00:09:59.724 --> 00:10:04.760
In general, there are two metrics which
LLM workloads care about, which is

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:07.800
throughput, which is really
just an economics thing.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:13.080
I buy a chip for $30,000,
and then can I do 10,000 tokens a second

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:15.400
or 100,000 tokens per
second of throughput?

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:17.400
That determines the dollars per token.

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:21.287
Throughput and then latency,
how fast does a thing respond?

00:10:21.371 --> 00:10:25.720
As I see the market, the economics
seems to be most important.

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:31.680
Ultimately,
the quality of the AI you can train

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:35.560
and serve is constrained by,
"I have only a $10 billion budget,

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:41.000
and I want to train and serve the best
model I can on that budget." If I can have

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:44.600
more tokens per dollar,
then I can get a better quality out.

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:51.560
The product we aim to build is far ahead
on throughput,

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:56.160
but then, actually,
the surprising thing is we're competitive

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:57.600
with the best on latency as well.

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:01.140
I think that is a unique thing
in offering both in the same place.

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:01.720
Is this for…

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:03.800
Obviously in AI,
there's training the models and then

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:05.206
running the models' inference.

00:11:05.290 --> 00:11:09.160
Is this most interesting for inference,
or is there any training angle?

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:14.280
Incidentally, is it useful for trading,
but you are trying to win inference,

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:15.680
is that how you think about it?

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:17.200
I think that's a reasonable
way to look at it.

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:23.080
I think the best inference chip today will
be a really good training chip as well.

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:27.320
Our product is both training
and inference, but I think the first

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:28.520
sales will be an inference.

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:33.000
That's mostly just a market effect where
it's easier to buy,

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:37.572
it's not as big of a risk to go to buy an
inference cluster as a training cluster.

00:11:38.072 --> 00:11:40.520
I think the product is really
compelling for training as well.

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:42.744
I think it should be
the best training product.

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:45.705
You guys just raised a big
new round of financing.

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:47.540
That's right.

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:54.720
We've raised a series B round, it's led
by Jane Street&lt;b&gt; and&lt;/b&gt; Situational Awareness.

00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:59.677
Situational Awareness, that is,
Leopold Aschenbrenner's fund.

00:11:59.761 --> 00:12:03.680
He wrote the definitive book on
AGI and where it's going.

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:07.040
Then Jane Street, they're
real technical experts.

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:09.200
They understand all
the details really well.

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:11.380
Very happy to be having
them lead the round.

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:18.800
It's a $500 million round, helps us
actually ramp the manufacturing and supply

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:21.720
chain for our chip so we can
bring our chip to market.

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:23.120
That's a lot of money.
It is.

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:28.920
I think
roughly, I would say it costs a ballpark

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:31.960
$100 million to produce
a chip in small volumes.

00:12:32.460 --> 00:12:37.640
But then you see the orders that are going
around, like OpenAI, Anthropic, Google.

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:40.360
They're going around buying
multi-gigawatt clusters, they cost

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:44.520
tens of billions of dollars of chips,
and you want to deploy all

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:46.120
of that in a year or so.

00:12:46.520 --> 00:12:49.268
You just need a massive
supply chain behind you.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:55.520
Assuming everything works technically,
what rate of production

00:12:55.520 --> 00:12:56.440
could you start to see?

00:12:57.560 --> 00:12:59.900
We have some estimates of where
we would like to be on this.

00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:05.840
Ramping to very large volumes
is a huge challenge for anyone.

00:13:06.200 --> 00:13:08.560
Obviously, for the large players,
they've had some practice at it.

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:11.520
Getting to a very large
volume for a startup is hard.

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:16.212
We would like to be at a place where
we're shipping multiple gigawatts a year.

00:13:16.295 --> 00:13:17.840
Multiple gigawatts per year?

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:18.840
Speaking of the metrics,
you talked about tokens per second.

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:19.340
Yeah.

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:24.560
We used to measure chips in FLOPS,
and I guess there's some kind

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:26.640
of custom FLOP thing for AI chips.

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:28.960
But is everyone just using
tokens per second these days?

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:31.728
Is the industry aligning
on that as the chip metric?

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:37.120
I guess it's like an application
metric versus the chip itself.

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:40.240
FLOPS of the chip is the key chip metric.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:44.640
There's a little bit of,
if I go and say,

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:50.200
"I've got an exaFLOP chip," to you then
the appropriate suspicion is to say,

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:54.800
"But can I actually use those FLOPS
effectively?" Then you need

00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:56.080
to map the application to that.

00:13:56.920 --> 00:14:00.715
This is kind of telling you the usable
FLOPS for your purposes.

00:14:00.798 --> 00:14:05.320
As a consumer of AI,
we have known for a long time

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:08.440
that lower latency products succeed.

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:17.040
Google talked about their internal testing
where the differences were down to…

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:18.840
Was it 50 milliseconds?
Something like that.

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:23.080
In result times where they noticed more
Google engagement, the faster the results

00:14:23.080 --> 00:14:27.680
were, and you'd think that 50
milliseconds is imperceptible to a human.

00:14:27.880 --> 00:14:29.400
It almost is, but turns out it's not.

00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:33.880
I think Amazon has,
certainly, they've optimized the latency

00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:35.560
of the Amazon experience quite a lot.

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:39.400
I don't know if they've talked about this
stuff publicly, but you know that their

00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:42.880
internal metrics similarly show
that the faster the product page loads,

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:47.400
the more people buy it.
Yet in AI, Google has carved out a

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:53.960
meaningful advantage via Gemini just being

00:14:53.960 --> 00:14:56.600
really fast for its level of intelligence.

00:14:57.880 --> 00:15:01.080
As far as I can tell,
ahead of most of the other labs

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:05.320
at a latency, at a fixed
high level of intelligence.

00:15:06.040 --> 00:15:12.760
Why have you guys or Groq or better chips
not been adopted faster

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:14.240
to give this product latency?

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:18.840
It's just that this will happen and you
guys will be powering all the AI products,

00:15:18.840 --> 00:15:22.213
but I note that Google has
an interesting lead there.

00:15:22.296 --> 00:15:23.880
I think there's ultimately…

00:15:24.520 --> 00:15:27.920
At least for existing chips in the market,
there's a really uncomfortable trade-off

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:29.280
between latency and throughput.

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:32.600
The chips that are best at throughput have
historically been the chips

00:15:32.600 --> 00:15:35.000
that are based on HBM as the memory.

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:39.000
That is Google, Amazon, NVIDIA.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:44.280
In order to have very large throughput,
you need a lot of inferences

00:15:44.280 --> 00:15:45.320
in flight simultaneously.

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:47.000
That needs the large memory.

00:15:47.071 --> 00:15:48.600
But that hasn't been so good at latency.

00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:51.280
Then, there's the Groq and Cerebras
that are much better at latency because

00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:55.320
they've got this, the SRAM,
weights are in SRAM, very low latency.

00:15:56.120 --> 00:15:59.680
The problem is, and the challenge
when you go to a Groq or Cerebras system

00:15:59.680 --> 00:16:03.280
is that the throughput you get there,
it just is not very good.

00:16:04.280 --> 00:16:08.920
The fundamental dollars per token is just
not competitive with Google

00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:11.262
or NVIDIA or Amazon.

00:16:11.345 --> 00:16:13.720
It is actually possible
to do both in the same chip.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:16.040
It's kind of an obvious thing.

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:17.520
You say you take the HBM,
you take the SRAM,

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:20.700
put them together on the same chip,
you put the weights in SRAM, and you put

00:16:21.400 --> 00:16:23.800
all of the inference data in HBM.

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:26.720
That is what we are doing, in fact.

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:29.600
I think that actually hits a really nice
sweet spot where you can get low

00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:31.000
latency and also be very cheap.

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:33.760
I think that's a really
attractive point to be.

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:36.880
It hasn't happened in the market yet,
just because of product decisions that

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:38.873
have been made by the different chips.

00:16:38.956 --> 00:16:43.040
But we should expect
all the AIs we're using to get

00:16:43.040 --> 00:16:45.160
significantly faster over
the coming 3–5 years?

00:16:45.160 --> 00:16:47.020
Order of magnitude faster, I'd say.

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:52.640
Generally, HBM-based chips tend to be
about 10 milliseconds or 20 milliseconds

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:55.200
per— I'm sorry, HBM-based
chips are things like TPUs?

00:16:55.200 --> 00:16:56.020
That's right.

00:16:57.720 --> 00:17:00.400
There's just some simple math of,
how long does it take you

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:02.800
to read through all of HBM?
It takes about 20 milliseconds.

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:06.280
That's the amount of time per token it
runs, whereas the amount of time to read

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:08.060
through all of SRAM is much faster.

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:11.120
You get about 1 millisecond,
so they are managed pretty faster.

00:17:11.360 --> 00:17:15.480
Famously, software used to be…

00:17:15.800 --> 00:17:19.200
Old-fashioned deterministic software,
the kind that's now out of favor,

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:22.960
used to be very easy and quick to scale.

00:17:23.320 --> 00:17:26.120
You had a social networks that have some
Southwest, Northwest moment,

00:17:26.120 --> 00:17:32.240
and they can scale through 10, 100,
1,000 orders of magnitude of adding users

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:35.720
because it's just a few rows
in a database, and it's

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:37.431
a very underutilized CPU.

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:39.560
What's interesting about the AI world

00:17:39.800 --> 00:17:45.080
is there are very real bottlenecks.

00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:48.320
Elon spent lots of time talking about
power, but it's not just

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:51.960
bringing power online.
You mentioned HBM is reminding me of…

00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:56.040
It seems like there's a view that maybe
there's going to be some HBM

00:17:56.760 --> 00:17:58.040
supply chain crunch.

00:17:59.662 --> 00:18:00.920
Where do you see…

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:05.960
Are we in for just a crunched world where
some limiter is

00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:11.400
pacing the rate of AI buildout over
the coming few years, where

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:14.440
the economics work,
and the products and everything like that,

00:18:14.440 --> 00:18:17.400
but ultimately,
we just can't bring the components online

00:18:17.400 --> 00:18:20.300
fast enough because we have to build
out the factories, things like that.

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:22.852
What are those crunched components?

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:24.800
I think so.

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:29.040
I'll just comment, by the way, this is a
great time to be a supplier in this place.

00:18:29.800 --> 00:18:31.840
Or just really— You should
have started an HBM company.

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:32.340
I know.

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:36.120
I think it's also just a fun time
to be someone who optimizes software.

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:38.120
That's always what I like doing.

00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:41.640
Always the challenge is,
"Why am I optimizing this if no one

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:46.680
cares?" But finally,
there's a place where you can…

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:49.260
It's actually very meaningful
in a very tangible sense.

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:54.520
If I can make this 20% more efficient,
then it can save that 20% of the buildout.

00:18:54.520 --> 00:18:57.840
The supply chain,
we're going to have crunches

00:18:57.840 --> 00:18:59.280
on all of the supply chain really.

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:05.000
If you look at the big components of what
any company, like us for example,

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:08.680
build out,
there is dependency on logic ties

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:13.960
from typically TSMC, maybe Samsung, or
HBM, which are the big three HBM vendors.

00:19:13.960 --> 00:19:16.040
Hynix, Samsung, and Micron.

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:20.360
Then there's also just the whole rack
manufacturing, which includes

00:19:22.120 --> 00:19:24.680
literally just sheet metal and so
on that builds the rack,

00:19:24.680 --> 00:19:28.640
but also cables and connectors because
of all the high-speed interconnect.

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:31.320
That's what we—Racks don't sound hard.

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:32.480
Are they sneaky hard?

00:19:33.297 --> 00:19:37.080
The big challenge is that you want
to bring in a huge amount of power,

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:41.880
get a huge amount of heat out,
and also have phenomenal interconnect,

00:19:41.880 --> 00:19:44.080
which has very high signal
integrity requirements.

00:19:44.960 --> 00:19:46.520
Pack a lot of cables in with…

00:19:46.520 --> 00:19:49.640
The cables don't bend too much,
they have to have enough copper in them,

00:19:49.640 --> 00:19:53.000
and so on, that you don't lose
data rate on the interconnect.

00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:56.220
If you push it to a limit, it's sound.

00:19:56.320 --> 00:19:58.823
Wafers, racks, HBM.
What else?

00:19:59.824 --> 00:20:03.800
Data centers, which I think is power,
primarily a little bit of buildout,

00:20:03.800 --> 00:20:07.248
but primarily power
and good infrastructure there.

00:20:08.600 --> 00:20:12.440
How do you then,
as a startup that is looking to acquire

00:20:12.440 --> 00:20:17.240
all these components,
elbow your way in amongst the giants

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:20.760
of the Googles and the NVIDIAs and all
these people who

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:24.960
have long-running relationships
and have been buying for much longer?

00:20:27.440 --> 00:20:31.400
Ultimately what all of these suppliers
care about, they do somewhat care about

00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:33.440
a diversity of their own customers.

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:35.800
It's not a great position to be in.

00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:37.760
They don't want monotony.
That's right, yeah.

00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:43.400
But then, what is their hesitation?

00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:46.400
Or the calculus for one of these large
suppliers is,

00:20:47.320 --> 00:20:51.120
if I reserve some of my capacity for you,
a startup, are you going

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:53.680
to be around in a year?
Is anyone going to even buy your product?

00:20:53.752 --> 00:20:59.520
Our approach has been to
just actually find buyers for the product,

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:02.970
and then the buyers answer
that question, ultimately.

00:21:04.040 --> 00:21:08.000
If you show up with a bunch of fairly
ironclad contracts to a supplier,

00:21:08.400 --> 00:21:09.960
then that has helped.

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:11.040
That's the nature of it, yeah.

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:15.240
I presume also
the round you just raised really helps

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:20.880
there, where showing that you are
incredibly well-capitalized and not going

00:21:20.880 --> 00:21:24.492
anywhere also helps from a supplier
validation point of view.

00:21:25.120 --> 00:21:25.640
Absolutely.

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:28.640
It helps just to say that we are around.

00:21:28.880 --> 00:21:30.400
We, in some cases, are actually…

00:21:31.280 --> 00:21:34.900
It depends on which part of the supply
chain, but some parts of the supply chain,

00:21:35.200 --> 00:21:38.080
some are fungible.
Logic ties are typically pretty fungible.

00:21:38.560 --> 00:21:42.480
But other parts of manufacturing,
you actually need something

00:21:42.480 --> 00:21:43.940
specifically set up for you.

00:21:44.320 --> 00:21:46.960
We're also able to cover
the capital costs of that.

00:21:47.056 --> 00:21:48.100
That makes sense.

00:21:48.320 --> 00:21:50.840
Coming back to the MatX architecture.

00:21:51.280 --> 00:21:54.640
You want to build the best chip for LLMs.
What is that?

00:21:54.640 --> 00:21:57.480
Yeah, exactly.
Sounds great.

00:21:58.984 --> 00:22:00.240
There's a few aspects to that.

00:22:00.240 --> 00:22:04.520
I think the first one is
pick your memory system right.

00:22:05.560 --> 00:22:08.600
I said, we've seen this HBM family,
we've got the SRAM family,

00:22:08.600 --> 00:22:12.160
put them both together is actually,
the most obvious idea,

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:13.480
but you can actually do it.

00:22:14.560 --> 00:22:16.240
There are a lot of details
to make that work well.

00:22:16.240 --> 00:22:17.120
We've done that work.

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:20.360
One of the things that shows up there is
you've spent all of this

00:22:20.360 --> 00:22:22.174
area on your chip on SRAM.

00:22:22.258 --> 00:22:25.080
How do you fit in the matrix multipliers,
which are the other big thing you really

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:28.600
need to do,
and somehow create a much more

00:22:28.600 --> 00:22:30.400
efficient matrix multiply engine?

00:22:31.280 --> 00:22:32.520
There is a gold standard for that.

00:22:32.520 --> 00:22:33.760
That is called the systolic array.

00:22:33.760 --> 00:22:35.080
Make a really large systolic array.

00:22:35.080 --> 00:22:37.440
You can't beat that in area
or power efficiency.

00:22:37.898 --> 00:22:39.520
Provably so?
Practically?

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:42.320
Practically.
It has not known a better approach there.

00:22:43.040 --> 00:22:45.840
The main thing is,
where are the inefficiencies typically?

00:22:45.840 --> 00:22:48.760
The inefficiencies show up when
you leave the systolic array.

00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:54.240
If you make a systolic array really big,
then you just don't leave it as often.

00:22:54.800 --> 00:22:55.600
That's the idea.

00:22:56.240 --> 00:22:57.720
Make a really big systolic array.

00:22:57.720 --> 00:23:04.480
That is sort of the theme of several
of the 2023-era startups, including us.

00:23:06.080 --> 00:23:10.440
But one of the challenges there is, now,
there is this part of the neural network,

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:13.520
as part of the Transformer,
which is this attention that doesn't

00:23:13.520 --> 00:23:16.240
map well onto a large systolic array.
That's the tension.

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:21.200
The mixture of expert layer maps really
well, but the attention does not.

00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:25.280
What we came up with,
which is quite different than some

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:29.000
of the other startups in this space,
is say, take a really large historical

00:23:29.000 --> 00:23:32.400
array, but have a way to split it up
into pieces without losing efficiency.

00:23:34.200 --> 00:23:36.960
That is the core of the design for us.

00:23:37.840 --> 00:23:39.400
Then, the third component.

00:23:39.640 --> 00:23:42.800
First was HBM and SRAM,
second is the systolic array,

00:23:42.880 --> 00:23:48.240
third component is an interesting new
approach on low-precision arithmetic.

00:23:48.240 --> 00:23:51.920
Low-precision arithmetic, in general,
we've seen number formats

00:23:51.920 --> 00:23:53.040
get narrower and narrower.

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:57.102
They get faster and faster as
you make them less precise.

00:23:57.186 --> 00:23:59.688
Number formats get narrower.
What does that mean?

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:04.109
Float32 was how people
used to train neural nets.

00:24:04.600 --> 00:24:06.240
That's just too much precision.
It's wasteful.

00:24:06.240 --> 00:24:07.120
Too much precision, yeah.

00:24:07.120 --> 00:24:12.320
It's like saying,
"I've got an image with a billion

00:24:12.320 --> 00:24:14.200
color bit depth." It's too many colors.

00:24:15.880 --> 00:24:18.160
You'd rather have more
pixels and fewer colors.

00:24:18.160 --> 00:24:22.840
That trend seems to go
almost all the way down to one bit even,

00:24:23.400 --> 00:24:28.360
where just have very few colors
but a huge number of pixels.

00:24:30.200 --> 00:24:35.099
In net seems to be a better,
just more efficient way to train models.

00:24:35.683 --> 00:24:41.800
Sorry, literally what precision
are you dealing with in this sense?

00:24:42.400 --> 00:24:43.160
We have a range.

00:24:48.920 --> 00:24:52.040
We actually have an ML team who we hired
specifically to research

00:24:52.520 --> 00:24:56.360
different forms of numerics and how to
make them all work together really well.

00:24:58.200 --> 00:24:59.960
We have a range of precisions.

00:24:59.960 --> 00:25:01.400
It's not just one precision.

00:25:01.400 --> 00:25:04.680
We think probably the main thing will be
similar to where NVIDIA is at,

00:25:04.680 --> 00:25:05.720
which is 4-bit precision.

00:25:06.280 --> 00:25:10.160
But I think a mix of different precisions
is useful for just when you look

00:25:10.160 --> 00:25:12.600
at the research, sometimes you want some
layers in higher precision or

00:25:12.600 --> 00:25:15.320
lower precision, and so on.
4-bit is 16.

00:25:15.800 --> 00:25:17.280
Yeah, you get 16 choices.
That's it.

00:25:17.600 --> 00:25:19.560
That's it.
It's pretty imprecise.

00:25:21.160 --> 00:25:21.880
That's really interesting.

00:25:21.880 --> 00:25:24.080
I didn't know about that dynamic,
but it makes sense.

00:25:24.080 --> 00:25:26.340
Half of them are positive,
half of them are negative.

00:25:26.760 --> 00:25:31.640
It's even— How do you design a chip?

00:25:33.640 --> 00:25:34.680
Is that a whiteboard?

00:25:34.880 --> 00:25:37.440
What software are you working in?
I'd just love to know…

00:25:37.440 --> 00:25:40.280
I understand how you design software
and what that process looks like.

00:25:40.280 --> 00:25:42.916
I have actually no sense
for what chip design looks like.

00:25:43.480 --> 00:25:47.320
The way that you actually type a chip
into a computer is similar to software.

00:25:47.520 --> 00:25:48.440
You write Verilog.

00:25:49.480 --> 00:25:50.880
Verilog is a programming language.

00:25:50.880 --> 00:25:53.320
It is a very parallel programming
language, which makes it different

00:25:53.320 --> 00:25:55.560
than C or Python or something.

00:25:56.440 --> 00:25:58.640
But it is a programming language.

00:25:59.560 --> 00:26:02.600
The mechanics of how you express
the design are the same as software,

00:26:02.680 --> 00:26:05.480
and we have continuous integration,
Git, all of those things.

00:26:05.522 --> 00:26:07.640
But a program executes...

00:26:07.880 --> 00:26:09.320
Like your Verilog program...

00:26:09.985 --> 00:26:11.620
We don't really run it.

00:26:11.720 --> 00:26:12.920
Exactly.
How does it run?

00:26:13.405 --> 00:26:15.000
We synthesize it.

00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:17.910
Synopsys and Cadence provide EDA tools.
EDA?

00:26:18.410 --> 00:26:23.832
You have to remember, I'm just—
I don't even know what it means, really.

00:26:24.416 --> 00:26:26.560
I think it's Electronic Design Automation.

00:26:27.040 --> 00:26:29.280
It takes the Verilog and says…

00:26:30.880 --> 00:26:33.960
First turns it into a description of what
are the logic gates that are involved,

00:26:33.960 --> 00:26:36.720
ANDs, ORs, NOTs, and then
the wires between them.

00:26:37.720 --> 00:26:42.851
Then it runs for days doing some really
difficult algorithms,

00:26:42.935 --> 00:26:45.120
and then eventually produces…

00:26:46.520 --> 00:26:48.360
Gates are the first thing,
and then even below that,

00:26:48.360 --> 00:26:50.440
it literally just produces polygons.

00:26:50.440 --> 00:26:53.040
It says like,
P-type semiconductor here, N-type

00:26:53.040 --> 00:26:55.239
semiconductor here, and polysilicon.

00:26:57.400 --> 00:27:02.600
You write Verilog,
and then that compiles down into gates

00:27:02.600 --> 00:27:06.760
and ultimately the Minecraft 3D.

00:27:07.960 --> 00:27:09.960
"This is where your elements should go."

00:27:11.400 --> 00:27:16.160
But then, what is the iteration loop?

00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:20.000
When we write code at Stripe,
we build a first version of something,

00:27:20.480 --> 00:27:24.977
and then we try it out, and then we
refine it, and we add more

00:27:25.060 --> 00:27:26.000
functionality over time.

00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:27.680
We're going to write
some tests at some point.

00:27:27.680 --> 00:27:31.200
We'll ship that, we'll find product market
fit, and then we'll refine it in market.

00:27:32.640 --> 00:27:35.700
Do you just sit down and write the
completed chip, and it works really well?

00:27:35.840 --> 00:27:37.800
Every year we tape-out a chip,
and if there's a bug,

00:27:37.800 --> 00:27:38.800
we just wait till next year.

00:27:38.800 --> 00:27:40.740
It's not really how we do it.
What's the iterative process?

00:27:41.200 --> 00:27:44.000
How do we actually do it?
It's much more waterfall than software is.

00:27:44.720 --> 00:27:47.220
Waterfall is almost a bad
word in software development.

00:27:47.280 --> 00:27:48.960
But it's a fact of life in general.

00:27:51.280 --> 00:27:57.280
The waterfall goes from architects to
logic designers who are writing Verilog.

00:27:57.800 --> 00:28:00.560
Then, there's this design verification,
and then physical design.

00:28:02.040 --> 00:28:05.000
There's this really big architecture phase
which happens before even writing any

00:28:05.000 --> 00:28:10.800
Verilog,
which is, "What do I want the organization

00:28:10.800 --> 00:28:13.680
of my chip to be?" In some sense…

00:28:13.680 --> 00:28:17.600
I came to hardware after doing
almost 10 years in software.

00:28:18.400 --> 00:28:20.880
I really like the blank
slate you get in hardware.

00:28:21.200 --> 00:28:23.600
You've got all of the raw materials,
you have a much more

00:28:24.560 --> 00:28:26.340
varied in what you have available.

00:28:27.520 --> 00:28:28.840
What is the organization of your chip?

00:28:28.840 --> 00:28:30.800
Do I have 100 cores?
Do I have one core?

00:28:31.360 --> 00:28:33.800
Do I have systolic arrays?
Do I have vector units?

00:28:33.800 --> 00:28:34.980
All of those things.

00:28:36.080 --> 00:28:38.760
Then we spend a long time coming up
with that general principle

00:28:39.240 --> 00:28:42.320
and then saying, "Okay, now I've got
these applications I want to run.

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:44.240
I want to run a transformer
of a particular shape.

00:28:44.240 --> 00:28:47.400
I want to map that onto this architecture
that I've got in my head."

00:28:48.680 --> 00:28:49.720
We do a lot of iteration.

00:28:50.120 --> 00:28:51.680
Well, I've got this
architecture in my head.

00:28:51.680 --> 00:28:53.800
I write it down to communicate to other
people, but that's just

00:28:53.800 --> 00:28:54.840
like a markdown file.

00:28:57.800 --> 00:29:01.200
Then, still actually a lot in my head,
but maybe with Python simulation and so

00:29:01.200 --> 00:29:04.760
on, I'll see,
do my applications map well to it?

00:29:05.200 --> 00:29:06.536
Can I run LLMs?

00:29:07.680 --> 00:29:12.880
You have a simulator where you
write your chip, you can then simulate its

00:29:12.880 --> 00:29:15.600
performance,
and you have some battery of tests

00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:18.240
that you see how this chip design works.

00:29:18.400 --> 00:29:20.560
Is it like an industry standard...

00:29:22.160 --> 00:29:24.972
Is it the X-Plane of chip testing?

00:29:28.280 --> 00:29:31.640
There's an industry standard thing for
the Verilog once you've done the design.

00:29:32.360 --> 00:29:34.760
They're just Verilog simulators
that you can test against.

00:29:37.160 --> 00:29:39.680
But you've already invested a huge amount
of work by the time you've got

00:29:39.680 --> 00:29:43.340
to that point, and so you sure hope you
haven't made a big mistake at that point.

00:29:44.840 --> 00:29:47.880
The thing that everyone does
prior to that is,

00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:52.600
we'll write our own performance simulator,
which, I mean,

00:29:53.000 --> 00:29:56.080
it is very specific to your particular
architecture, and you can write it quite

00:29:56.080 --> 00:29:58.440
concisely in just a normal
programming language.

00:29:59.160 --> 00:30:01.240
That is where most of the
architecture work is done.

00:30:01.240 --> 00:30:03.880
Then the simulation on Verilog is more,
"I know what I'm doing.

00:30:03.880 --> 00:30:07.400
I just want to make sure I didn't have any
bugs when I implemented it." But I presume

00:30:07.400 --> 00:30:10.000
it's a game of inches where different
people are trying different

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:11.800
things, and then you...

00:30:13.800 --> 00:30:18.440
Do you simulate it to see if it runs
1% better across the battery of tests?

00:30:18.440 --> 00:30:19.820
Or is that not how it works?

00:30:20.027 --> 00:30:21.400
In this space, not so much.

00:30:22.480 --> 00:30:27.840
Just to characterize what performance
of an AI chip is, it is how many, really…

00:30:29.000 --> 00:30:30.520
First thing you care about is FLOPS.

00:30:31.160 --> 00:30:32.480
How many FLOPS have I got?

00:30:32.480 --> 00:30:37.120
That's a product of how many multiplies,
like, "I've got a grid of a certain size,

00:30:37.120 --> 00:30:41.160
1000 by 1000." Can do a million
multiplies in a clock cycle.

00:30:41.440 --> 00:30:43.560
Then I have a certain clock
frequency, a gigahertz.

00:30:43.880 --> 00:30:45.960
I multiply them out.
That is the speed of it.

00:30:47.000 --> 00:30:50.600
I don't even need to write
that and test it to see how fast it is.

00:30:50.600 --> 00:30:51.160
It just is.

00:30:52.680 --> 00:30:55.000
What I plan in advance is
it's going to be this fast.

00:30:55.560 --> 00:30:58.480
What I can then optimize on,
maybe a little bit, is clock speed.

00:30:58.480 --> 00:31:00.220
There's not a lot I can do there.

00:31:00.960 --> 00:31:02.920
Then, I can optimize
a bit on area as well.

00:31:03.200 --> 00:31:06.300
There is some room for optimization,
but actually a lot of it gets set.

00:31:06.600 --> 00:31:10.035
Actually, just the speed of the chip
gets set very much upfront.

00:31:11.000 --> 00:31:13.600
Then how many chips do you fab?

00:31:13.600 --> 00:31:19.160
Is it only the ones going into production,
or is it just build a few to throw away,

00:31:19.160 --> 00:31:20.120
or how does it work?

00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:24.480
The ideal, which companies tend to hit
about 50% of the time,

00:31:24.480 --> 00:31:27.040
is that your first tape-out…

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:28.680
Tape-out costs $30 million.

00:31:28.845 --> 00:31:30.800
Your first— Tape-out
is just production run?

00:31:30.800 --> 00:31:31.740
That's right.

00:31:33.080 --> 00:31:37.000
The actual manual, the first chip costs
$30 million, the second chip costs $1,000.

00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:37.500
Yes.

00:31:38.600 --> 00:31:40.600
Tape-out is that first chip.
Okay.

00:31:40.600 --> 00:31:44.120
The ideal is that your first tape-out
is actually your production thing.

00:31:44.600 --> 00:31:48.080
You do a tape-out, you
make maybe a thousand chips and test them,

00:31:48.080 --> 00:31:49.560
and then you do production volume.

00:31:50.920 --> 00:31:57.160
In the unlucky 50% of the time, you need
to redo some or all of your tape-out.

00:31:58.120 --> 00:32:02.160
In good cases, and in many cases,
you can redo just the metal layers

00:32:02.160 --> 00:32:04.600
which costs you only like $100,000.

00:32:05.240 --> 00:32:08.120
As opposed to the—
Pay the $30 million again.

00:32:09.520 --> 00:32:12.240
But in bad cases,
if you've made something serious and you

00:32:12.240 --> 00:32:14.720
can't fix it at the metal layers,
you have to do the whole thing again.

00:32:15.058 --> 00:32:17.040
Why can't that be solved?

00:32:18.240 --> 00:32:21.600
Is that definitionally an error
in simulation, where it turns out these

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:27.120
two gates were too close together, and
it just led to some reliability issues?

00:32:30.640 --> 00:32:30.800
Yeah.

00:32:30.800 --> 00:32:35.920
What you're describing is physical,
the physical implementation

00:32:35.920 --> 00:32:37.960
of the chip is wrong.
That's one class.

00:32:38.040 --> 00:32:41.501
The other class is that the logical
specification of the chip is wrong.

00:32:41.560 --> 00:32:44.120
But shouldn't that be—
Shouldn't you have caught that before?

00:32:44.680 --> 00:32:44.840
Yeah.

00:32:44.840 --> 00:32:46.920
Before you spent $30
million on manufacturing it.

00:32:46.920 --> 00:32:47.580
Yeah.

00:32:49.560 --> 00:32:52.520
We do a lot of testing.
We try not to ship these things.

00:32:53.720 --> 00:32:56.080
I hear software companies also
ship bugs to production as well.

00:32:56.080 --> 00:32:56.700
Fair.

00:32:57.480 --> 00:33:00.000
Sometimes things— It's a very good retort.

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:01.400
Shouldn't you not be shipping bugs?

00:33:03.600 --> 00:33:06.280
But there is a real trade-off in,
you can spend more and more

00:33:06.280 --> 00:33:07.520
time on design verification.

00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:12.699
There's always this question of,
when do you stop?

00:33:14.240 --> 00:33:18.400
You stop when your coverage metrics ever
hit a certain point, but maybe not 100%.

00:33:19.160 --> 00:33:19.840
Then if

00:33:22.720 --> 00:33:27.920
Apple has to discretize the iPhone release

00:33:27.920 --> 00:33:31.800
cycle, and they have settled on once per
year, they'll decide,

00:33:31.800 --> 00:33:34.400
"We've got this better camera,
but it's got to wait for the next

00:33:34.400 --> 00:33:38.640
version," or, "We're going to improve
the waterproofing, but that's got to wait

00:33:38.640 --> 00:33:42.800
for the iPhone 8 or whatever."
They have taken a continuous process

00:33:42.800 --> 00:33:46.320
of always coming up with ways to make
the iPhone better and discretized

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:48.240
it into annual iPhone releases.

00:33:48.640 --> 00:33:51.821
What will your discrete cadence be?
What's our vision of that?

00:33:52.080 --> 00:33:52.322
Yeah.

00:33:52.322 --> 00:33:56.240
Many chip vendors have this sort
of tick-tock model, which is

00:33:56.880 --> 00:34:00.480
you'll do on one generation,
maybe you're trying to release every year.

00:34:01.880 --> 00:34:05.320
On even numbered years,
you'll do a physical technology upgrade,

00:34:05.320 --> 00:34:09.080
so new transistor technology, new
memory technology, and you interconnect.

00:34:09.680 --> 00:34:12.600
Then on odd numbered years,
you might do an architecture overhaul.

00:34:12.600 --> 00:34:16.640
I think that's a pretty good fit because
you have different parts of your company

00:34:16.640 --> 00:34:19.760
that are skilled at different areas,
and it allows you to keep both of them

00:34:19.760 --> 00:34:25.600
occupied without having instead every
2 years doing a massive risk release.

00:34:25.600 --> 00:34:26.220
Yeah.

00:34:26.640 --> 00:34:28.040
So you think that's
probably likely for you?

00:34:28.040 --> 00:34:28.940
That's right.

00:34:29.960 --> 00:34:31.500
You mentioned interconnect.

00:34:31.880 --> 00:34:34.320
There's a narrative out there
that in NVIDIA,

00:34:34.320 --> 00:34:37.520
a huge part of the defensibility comes not
from the chips, which are good,

00:34:37.520 --> 00:34:42.600
but from the software layer,
and the ability for engineers to write

00:34:42.600 --> 00:34:46.680
these really parallel workloads,
and the fact that they've been refining

00:34:46.680 --> 00:34:49.240
CUDA for whatever number of years.
A decade or so.

00:34:49.240 --> 00:34:51.160
Exactly, a long time.

00:34:52.120 --> 00:34:56.469
How do you think about parallelization,
and is that narrative true?

00:34:56.553 --> 00:34:57.900
It's true for sure.

00:34:58.200 --> 00:35:02.040
It's true in many areas of the market.

00:35:03.160 --> 00:35:06.840
I think,
and especially where you look at where

00:35:06.840 --> 00:35:09.480
NVIDIA entered the market, they're doing

00:35:11.320 --> 00:35:15.000
PC devices, lots of gaming, and so on.

00:35:15.000 --> 00:35:18.760
There are thousands of games,
maybe tens of thousands of games released,

00:35:19.080 --> 00:35:22.320
and they all need to be programmed against
CUDA, and so

00:35:23.640 --> 00:35:27.040
there's such a huge investment
in the software that this is really

00:35:27.040 --> 00:35:28.120
important, the compatibility.

00:35:28.585 --> 00:35:30.320
There are not thousands of LLMs.

00:35:30.320 --> 00:35:33.800
There's one LLM per frontier lab,
and there's maybe five frontier

00:35:33.800 --> 00:35:35.300
labs or something like that.

00:35:37.720 --> 00:35:39.600
Just the economics of that is different.

00:35:39.846 --> 00:35:45.360
The calculation for a frontier lab roughly
goes as, I just bought a $10 billion

00:35:45.840 --> 00:35:49.760
compute cluster,
I have hired 50 of the best

00:35:50.240 --> 00:35:55.195
people who can write optimized GPU,
or TPU, or Trainium software.

00:35:56.960 --> 00:35:59.440
I pay them less than
$10 billion, a lot less.

00:36:01.680 --> 00:36:05.200
Let's put them to work
optimizing the compute.

00:36:07.440 --> 00:36:10.360
Good work there,
depends on what your baseline is,

00:36:10.360 --> 00:36:13.760
but it can very easily double
the performance of the software you write.

00:36:15.080 --> 00:36:18.680
There is a huge amount of custom software
written for every generation of chip.

00:36:19.080 --> 00:36:22.640
When a new chip comes out, software is
substantially rewritten

00:36:22.640 --> 00:36:24.280
to optimize for that specific chip.

00:36:24.560 --> 00:36:27.880
That's just the right trade-off given
the relative costs of these things.

00:36:28.440 --> 00:36:32.840
What that means for us is
that ecosystem already exists,

00:36:33.640 --> 00:36:37.320
and that way of operating, where you say,
"I'm just going to staff a 50-person team

00:36:39.240 --> 00:36:42.840
to write software for this chip,"
works really well if you're

00:36:42.840 --> 00:36:44.540
trying to sell to frontier labs.

00:36:44.600 --> 00:36:49.240
You're saying CUDA is way more important
for the games environment,

00:36:49.240 --> 00:36:55.800
where it just does a lot of games
than this top-heavy AI market that we're

00:36:55.800 --> 00:37:02.000
in, where
if people say, "You need to then customize

00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:06.599
your workload for a MatX chip," it's like,
"Well, fine, do that." Cost of business.

00:37:07.100 --> 00:37:09.400
Yeah, that makes a lot of sense.

00:37:11.080 --> 00:37:13.600
Where will you fab the chips?
TSMC.

00:37:16.520 --> 00:37:20.600
Why is TSMC so durable?

00:37:22.200 --> 00:37:23.200
It's interesting.

00:37:23.200 --> 00:37:24.800
They don't charge a lot as well.

00:37:24.800 --> 00:37:26.880
You'd think that if they're a monopoly
provider, they should

00:37:26.880 --> 00:37:28.760
charge a lot of money.
They don't.

00:37:29.640 --> 00:37:32.280
I think that is a big aspect
of why they're so durable.

00:37:32.625 --> 00:37:36.400
It's like this
cyclical conservatism

00:37:36.880 --> 00:37:41.080
crossed with Taiwanese business
conservatism, means you're at the most

00:37:41.080 --> 00:37:43.600
conservative part of the matrix.

00:37:43.920 --> 00:37:45.096
But I mean, it does…

00:37:48.480 --> 00:37:51.280
I mean, an American capitalist might say,
"Well, they're just screwing up.

00:37:51.280 --> 00:37:54.120
They could have extracted more money
from the market." But you could also say

00:37:54.120 --> 00:37:59.800
that this is actually the long-term
sustaining advantage because they will

00:37:59.800 --> 00:38:01.120
just stay ahead for a really long time.

00:38:01.120 --> 00:38:03.060
They don't incur
the creation of competitors.

00:38:03.360 --> 00:38:06.920
But isn't the creation of competitors
priced in because

00:38:06.920 --> 00:38:08.040
of the geopolitical risk?

00:38:10.160 --> 00:38:13.800
It's not like everyone's fat, dumb,
and happy with their TSMC dependence.

00:38:13.800 --> 00:38:15.420
They're actually thinking a lot about it.

00:38:15.480 --> 00:38:17.760
So there is real technical
advantage there as well.

00:38:17.760 --> 00:38:19.320
It's not just the discouragement.

00:38:19.589 --> 00:38:23.840
But designing chips seems really hard,
building airplanes seems really hard.

00:38:23.920 --> 00:38:30.520
There are so many areas where competitive
market forces create multiple options.

00:38:31.800 --> 00:38:33.478
Yet, that has not occurred here.

00:38:34.080 --> 00:38:35.240
There are multiple options.

00:38:35.480 --> 00:38:37.000
You can buy from Intel or Samsung.

00:38:37.720 --> 00:38:38.960
But at leading-edge nodes.

00:38:40.320 --> 00:38:42.480
What do we even care about
in leading-edge nodes, I guess?

00:38:42.480 --> 00:38:43.800
The big advantage is on power.

00:38:44.440 --> 00:38:46.280
The advantage on area is smaller.

00:38:46.440 --> 00:38:49.800
The leading edge nodes, the density
doesn't go up as much as it used to.

00:38:52.120 --> 00:38:54.880
When you are really,
really sensitive to power, it is a good

00:38:54.880 --> 00:38:56.120
idea to be on leading edge nodes.

00:38:56.120 --> 00:38:57.880
That is AI chips and mobile phone chips.

00:38:59.320 --> 00:39:02.360
But there's a lot of the market where you
don't actually like

00:39:02.360 --> 00:39:03.740
the devices in your car.
Sure.

00:39:04.040 --> 00:39:05.320
Car chips, yeah, that's fine.

00:39:05.960 --> 00:39:09.138
But you're saying,
if you exclude the two most interesting

00:39:09.222 --> 00:39:16.160
parts of the market— That's true.

00:39:16.160 --> 00:39:17.480
For this super high-growth area
of the market, it's interesting to me,

00:39:17.480 --> 00:39:22.120
again, there's a lot of other
really complex business problems out

00:39:22.120 --> 00:39:25.600
there that competition has solved.
Chip design is a…

00:39:25.600 --> 00:39:28.720
Like, why has someone not left
TSMC and gone and built a new fab?

00:39:29.960 --> 00:39:30.800
I don't know.

00:39:32.400 --> 00:39:35.280
The cost of a fab is extremely expensive.

00:39:35.360 --> 00:39:38.640
I recognize that also the cost
of a lab is extremely expensive, too.

00:39:41.840 --> 00:39:44.560
I don't really understand the technical
details of why it's so hard.

00:39:45.440 --> 00:39:49.920
I mean, there is some amount of just a $10
billion fab versus $100 million

00:39:51.280 --> 00:39:52.680
tape-out and chip development.

00:39:52.680 --> 00:39:53.680
There's a huge difference there.

00:39:53.680 --> 00:39:55.040
But beyond that, I'm not sure.

00:39:55.351 --> 00:39:58.000
What's TSMC like to deal with?
They're very big.

00:39:58.440 --> 00:40:03.360
As a startup, we tend to work with,
not directly with TSMC, but with

00:40:04.240 --> 00:40:07.560
an ASIC vendor who,
firstly, does a huge amount of the actual

00:40:07.560 --> 00:40:10.840
backend work for us to interface
with them, but then also has their

00:40:10.840 --> 00:40:12.580
existing relationships with them.

00:40:13.440 --> 00:40:16.960
TSMC cares a lot about diversity
of their customer pool.

00:40:18.124 --> 00:40:20.160
It gets back to that conservatism.
Yeah.

00:40:21.120 --> 00:40:23.060
They're great to work
with from that perspective.

00:40:24.160 --> 00:40:26.100
They want to encourage startups.
That's right.

00:40:26.240 --> 00:40:29.120
That's very cool.
Why don't the labs design their own chips?

00:40:29.480 --> 00:40:30.720
Google does.
Google does.

00:40:31.040 --> 00:40:32.597
OpenAI is starting.

00:40:32.960 --> 00:40:35.560
It's really a trade-off of how much
advantage do you get from vertical

00:40:35.560 --> 00:40:40.080
integration versus how much advantage
do you get by concentration of R&amp;D work.

00:40:41.440 --> 00:40:43.840
You take the five labs,
and if they all buy from one player,

00:40:43.840 --> 00:40:46.560
then you can put five times
as much R&amp;D into that chip.

00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:51.840
Does that beat the advantage you get from
saying, "I know exactly what my model is"?

00:40:52.825 --> 00:40:57.200
Because of the several-years delay
from designing a chip to being

00:40:57.200 --> 00:40:59.960
in production,
you can't actually say,

00:40:59.960 --> 00:41:04.560
"I know exactly what my model is," because
models change much faster than that.

00:41:06.080 --> 00:41:09.040
Even the labs are forced into this
position where they have to make

00:41:09.040 --> 00:41:11.480
predictions, and they have to hedge
against what they might

00:41:11.480 --> 00:41:12.400
do two years from now.

00:41:12.480 --> 00:41:16.200
The calculus is, what is the probability
distribution of what my model might look

00:41:16.200 --> 00:41:20.840
like and then
design a chip that gets 90% of that

00:41:20.840 --> 00:41:22.540
probability distribution or something?

00:41:23.200 --> 00:41:26.360
Elon is excited about
data centers in space.

00:41:27.040 --> 00:41:33.320
The two criticisms I've heard are that
cooling is very hard and then

00:41:34.360 --> 00:41:36.520
repairing the chips is hard.

00:41:37.120 --> 00:41:39.497
But I know nothing about chips.
You do.

00:41:41.040 --> 00:41:42.520
The repair is really interesting.

00:41:43.880 --> 00:41:47.480
When you look at how NVIDIA deploys their
racks, how we do something pretty

00:41:47.480 --> 00:41:48.680
similar to what NVIDIA does.

00:41:50.160 --> 00:41:52.880
In general, you always need to design
for the fact that some of your

00:41:52.880 --> 00:41:53.600
chips are going to be down.

00:41:53.600 --> 00:41:57.240
Mean time between failure
of chips is not that large.

00:41:58.120 --> 00:42:01.000
In a cluster of 100,000 chips,
there are going to be chips

00:42:01.000 --> 00:42:02.380
that are down all the time.

00:42:02.520 --> 00:42:07.358
One way you can do that is you can make a
rack where one rack has spare chips in it.

00:42:07.442 --> 00:42:11.280
NVIDIA has eight spare
chips in a rack of 64.

00:42:13.040 --> 00:42:14.220
That's pretty good.

00:42:15.040 --> 00:42:17.080
The combinatorics works
really well for you there.

00:42:20.480 --> 00:42:27.000
Because you can pick which ones to avoid,
you can with very high probability

00:42:27.000 --> 00:42:27.960
tolerate a lot of failures.

00:42:27.960 --> 00:42:31.480
And then the other just for the other
family of things is to say my rack has to

00:42:31.480 --> 00:42:33.660
work, but I have some spare racks as well.

00:42:35.200 --> 00:42:38.400
You can math that out with
the tax of reliability here is

00:42:38.400 --> 00:42:39.760
only like 10%, that's pretty good.

00:42:41.760 --> 00:42:45.920
But that relies on someone coming
and servicing the part within

00:42:45.920 --> 00:42:46.960
a day or something like that.

00:42:46.960 --> 00:42:49.440
If you say they're going to service it
never, then

00:42:50.160 --> 00:42:53.440
I think you actually can get where you
want to be, but maybe with 100%

00:42:53.440 --> 00:42:55.040
tax on reliability rather than 10%.

00:42:55.760 --> 00:42:59.600
For example, if you think the average
lifetime of a chip is in the range

00:42:59.600 --> 00:43:00.980
of three to five years.

00:43:01.560 --> 00:43:05.120
That means if I deploy twice as many
chips, then three to five years from now,

00:43:05.120 --> 00:43:06.580
half of them will still work.

00:43:06.880 --> 00:43:10.840
Also, the burn-in is
particularly failure-y.

00:43:10.840 --> 00:43:12.840
How about the cooling?
Most of the challenge...

00:43:14.200 --> 00:43:17.080
I guess this is actually really
a data center design aspect.

00:43:18.680 --> 00:43:20.880
At the rack level,
the challenge of cooling is just getting

00:43:20.880 --> 00:43:24.760
the heat out as quickly as possible out
of the rack into the cooling network.

00:43:26.520 --> 00:43:30.280
How you get it out of the spaceship,
other people would know

00:43:30.280 --> 00:43:31.567
that better than I do.

00:43:33.080 --> 00:43:36.989
That seems to be the main
objection, but I don't know.

00:43:37.573 --> 00:43:40.680
I think it's
if you think the cost of repair is

00:43:40.680 --> 00:43:43.720
that you need to have deployed twice as
many chips, then it's a trade-off

00:43:43.720 --> 00:43:46.499
of the capital of the chips
versus the power saving.

00:43:46.582 --> 00:43:51.000
Exactly, the repair thing, it feels like,
can be solved, because also,

00:43:51.720 --> 00:43:54.360
I think part of the bet,
part of Elon's claim,

00:43:54.360 --> 00:43:58.720
is that we will just be so power-limited
that you have no option

00:43:58.720 --> 00:43:59.960
but to go to space.

00:44:00.280 --> 00:44:05.480
People can argue about that,
but were that to be the case, then yes,

00:44:05.480 --> 00:44:08.560
it's like you can get power in space,
and you cannot on Earth,

00:44:08.840 --> 00:44:10.560
so you might as well go there.

00:44:10.560 --> 00:44:13.480
Whereas like the cooling is a more
fundamental, "Does the product actually

00:44:13.480 --> 00:44:19.280
work at all?" Reiner thinks about AI

00:44:19.280 --> 00:44:20.960
the unglamorous way: compute,

00:44:20.960 --> 00:44:24.320
systems architecture, and what it
takes to run models reliably at scale.

00:44:24.680 --> 00:44:27.920
If you're building an AI product,
the business model similarly has

00:44:27.920 --> 00:44:29.680
a ton of unglamorous complexity.

00:44:29.680 --> 00:44:32.400
You're not just selling AI,
you're monetizing consumption across

00:44:32.400 --> 00:44:35.520
API calls, tokens processed, GPU hours.

00:44:36.240 --> 00:44:39.240
Stripe Billing is a scalable
system for usage-based billing.

00:44:39.240 --> 00:44:41.920
It lets you launch token-based pricing,
subscriptions, credits,

00:44:41.920 --> 00:44:43.280
hybrid models, whatever you want.

00:44:43.800 --> 00:44:46.960
You can create revenue models based
on usage without rebuilding your

00:44:46.960 --> 00:44:48.400
pricing system every six months.

00:44:48.800 --> 00:44:51.981
If you're building an AI product,
Stripe Billing is worth a look.

00:44:55.067 --> 00:44:57.695
What are your AI predictions for 2026?

00:44:58.279 --> 00:45:03.040
What I'm really excited about is
just being able to…

00:45:04.080 --> 00:45:05.940
I'm still excited about the coding.

00:45:06.160 --> 00:45:07.600
This is what we do as a company.

00:45:07.600 --> 00:45:09.440
It's what many others
do as a company as well.

00:45:10.960 --> 00:45:14.880
The one aspect of this is
expanding into more domains.

00:45:16.160 --> 00:45:18.720
For example, in where we spend our time.

00:45:21.440 --> 00:45:24.640
We as a company, we write Rust,
we write Verilog, we write Python.

00:45:26.080 --> 00:45:28.000
No Haskell?
No, there's a story there.

00:45:28.880 --> 00:45:30.160
I used to love Haskell.

00:45:30.320 --> 00:45:31.680
Rust is my current favorite.

00:45:32.160 --> 00:45:33.200
Mutation is good.

00:45:36.560 --> 00:45:40.640
The models are extremely
good at Rust and Python.

00:45:40.720 --> 00:45:42.160
They've done a lot of RL on them.

00:45:42.960 --> 00:45:45.920
They have not done as much RL on Verilog.

00:45:46.560 --> 00:45:51.880
They've done almost none on,
"Write me a markdown file that describes

00:45:51.880 --> 00:45:55.080
a chip architecture."
How do you even RL on that?

00:45:55.080 --> 00:45:58.600
You have to say,
what is a good chip architecture?

00:45:58.600 --> 00:46:01.180
You have to somehow say whether
that's a good result or not.

00:46:02.000 --> 00:46:05.680
I think one of the things the labs are
doing is trying to broaden what they've

00:46:05.680 --> 00:46:08.680
done RL on, source it from customers

00:46:08.680 --> 00:46:15.523
and so on in order to make it less spiky,

00:46:15.640 --> 00:46:17.200
fill out the gaps between the spikes.

00:46:17.200 --> 00:46:22.000
I presume the labs would love to work
with you on improving the models

00:46:23.760 --> 00:46:28.160
by doing RL on this specific task.

00:46:28.244 --> 00:46:29.600
However, it's also special.

00:46:29.600 --> 00:46:31.000
How does that make sense for us?

00:46:31.000 --> 00:46:32.000
You're a special sauce.

00:46:33.120 --> 00:46:38.320
Do you want to come up with some AI
approaches but keep them proprietary?

00:46:40.480 --> 00:46:42.320
We've looked at a few
different aspects here.

00:46:42.320 --> 00:46:42.960
There's the…

00:46:43.640 --> 00:46:46.480
What we're able to do by ourselves,
our business is not training models.

00:46:46.480 --> 00:46:50.360
We do it in order to do the research
on numerics, but actual

00:46:50.360 --> 00:46:52.020
production models we don't do.

00:46:54.020 --> 00:46:59.200
The biggest mileage is on the RL, and it's
not something we can really do ourselves.

00:46:59.200 --> 00:47:02.800
We'd love it if we could have a custom
model just for us, but that doesn't

00:47:02.800 --> 00:47:05.280
seem to be— Well you could, right?

00:47:05.280 --> 00:47:08.659
The terms we've been offered by labs so
far have not been on those terms, but—

00:47:08.680 --> 00:47:10.280
Because you have to share the IP back.

00:47:10.578 --> 00:47:15.480
The way they prefer to do it is that
they put it into their mainstream

00:47:15.480 --> 00:47:17.000
model because it's good for them.

00:47:17.400 --> 00:47:19.180
Which, obviously, you don't want to do.

00:47:19.960 --> 00:47:20.840
How do you think…

00:47:21.720 --> 00:47:25.080
What does you using AI to design
a model do you think look like?

00:47:25.080 --> 00:47:29.520
Because this is actually an interesting
sight glass into a weak version

00:47:29.520 --> 00:47:34.320
of recursive self-improvement where we're
using the AIs to develop better AIs.

00:47:35.080 --> 00:47:37.480
I'm curious,
what you think that looks like?

00:47:37.480 --> 00:47:40.520
Is it your own proprietary
recursive models?

00:47:40.600 --> 00:47:41.320
What else?

00:47:41.560 --> 00:47:44.695
Is there day-to-day AI
usage that's load-bearing?

00:47:45.800 --> 00:47:48.840
The stuff that is available today and I
think will become even better,

00:47:49.480 --> 00:47:53.640
very quickly is just
the stuff that looks most like software.

00:47:54.120 --> 00:47:57.240
Writing Verilog, running tests,
running continuous integration, and so on.

00:48:00.720 --> 00:48:03.240
That is a big fraction
of the development time in a chip.

00:48:03.240 --> 00:48:05.240
It's probably 9, 12, 15 months.

00:48:05.240 --> 00:48:10.440
There's some stuff that's downstream
of that, which is physical design,

00:48:10.680 --> 00:48:16.519
which is, you take that Verilog and
you generate the gates and the polygons.

00:48:17.120 --> 00:48:18.520
We don't have a clear path for…

00:48:19.760 --> 00:48:23.440
It's not at least the most obvious thing
is not clear for how to compress that.

00:48:24.880 --> 00:48:26.560
The goal, can you tape-out
a chip in one month?

00:48:26.560 --> 00:48:28.100
One month would be the goal.

00:48:28.400 --> 00:48:30.800
In theory, you could compress all
of the logic design and design

00:48:30.800 --> 00:48:35.000
verification down to a short amount
of time just by continuing

00:48:35.000 --> 00:48:36.720
on the same path we're doing now.

00:48:36.720 --> 00:48:39.520
But if you wanted to take
the physical design down,

00:48:39.600 --> 00:48:42.840
that has to leave code,
you're now doing like graphical interfaces

00:48:42.840 --> 00:48:45.047
and saying I want to play stuff and so on.

00:48:45.548 --> 00:48:47.680
Actually, there has been work on this even

00:48:47.680 --> 00:48:52.720
prior to LLMs, which is a specific

00:48:53.600 --> 00:48:55.520
model trained for that particular problem.

00:48:56.360 --> 00:48:59.360
I think the vendors,
which is like Synopsys and Cadence,

00:49:00.400 --> 00:49:02.640
probably should move in that direction.

00:49:03.200 --> 00:49:05.560
Most of the focus has not been,
do it faster; it's been,

00:49:05.560 --> 00:49:06.640
do it with higher quality.

00:49:08.480 --> 00:49:12.400
But that is a big bottleneck on,
can I have a new chip every month?

00:49:13.000 --> 00:49:16.200
There's just the practical thing of a new
chip every month doesn't really make

00:49:16.200 --> 00:49:17.760
sense because then if I'm deploying...

00:49:19.200 --> 00:49:21.760
If it takes me a year to populate a data
center, that means I'm going to have

00:49:21.760 --> 00:49:23.680
different chips in different
corners of the data center.

00:49:23.680 --> 00:49:24.180
Yes.

00:49:24.480 --> 00:49:28.480
When you talk about one month to tapeout,
so you do all this work

00:49:28.480 --> 00:49:31.200
to ultimately produce a file.

00:49:34.000 --> 00:49:38.480
Everything TSMC then does,
it's not entirely in software.

00:49:38.720 --> 00:49:42.360
Is there some type-setting that has
to happen of moving stuff around?

00:49:42.360 --> 00:49:44.680
But what happens when you
send your files to TSMC?

00:49:45.080 --> 00:49:47.080
Then what?
They create a mask.

00:49:47.520 --> 00:49:50.440
That is where the ASML tools come in.

00:49:52.440 --> 00:49:55.000
A mask is really just a stencil.

00:49:55.000 --> 00:49:59.000
You shoot the lasers through the mask or
the x-rays through the mask and then

00:50:00.040 --> 00:50:04.640
that produces the different
P-type and N-type semiconductors.

00:50:05.560 --> 00:50:08.020
They produced the mask.
That is the expensive part.

00:50:11.040 --> 00:50:13.600
They're building up these
15 or so metal layers.

00:50:14.240 --> 00:50:17.220
They placed on the silicon and then
there are different layers of metals

00:50:17.360 --> 00:50:19.280
which connect all
the transistors together.

00:50:20.960 --> 00:50:22.080
They do that on a wafer.

00:50:22.480 --> 00:50:24.960
It happens on a stepping basis.

00:50:25.760 --> 00:50:28.840
There's a maximum size of chip you can
build which is constrained

00:50:28.840 --> 00:50:29.760
by this machinery.

00:50:30.528 --> 00:50:34.031
The wafer stepper is part
of the ASML special sauce, right?

00:50:34.120 --> 00:50:37.451
There are probably some important
alignment requirement there.

00:50:38.035 --> 00:50:41.440
I think I remember that being quite like
the classic manufacturing

00:50:41.440 --> 00:50:42.360
throughput problem.

00:50:42.360 --> 00:50:44.620
I think they've done a lot
of work on optimizing that.

00:50:45.680 --> 00:50:49.560
They tape that, so then you just
produce hundreds of copies of your chip.

00:50:49.960 --> 00:50:52.280
You have to test it
because there are defects.

00:50:52.920 --> 00:50:58.040
You typically, I think the average rates
really depends on process and so on,

00:50:58.040 --> 00:51:01.400
but small single digit
number of defects per chip.

00:51:03.320 --> 00:51:05.800
You test the chip and see
whether it has any defects in it.

00:51:06.520 --> 00:51:10.040
Many chips are designed to be able
to tolerate a few defects, and so you need

00:51:10.040 --> 00:51:11.980
to configure it to tolerate the defects.

00:51:12.000 --> 00:51:13.960
Now you have a die that by itself works.

00:51:15.200 --> 00:51:16.120
Then you need to package it.

00:51:16.120 --> 00:51:19.680
You put it
in a package together with memories,

00:51:19.680 --> 00:51:24.520
typically that's the HBM, and then maybe
you escape the wires

00:51:24.520 --> 00:51:25.920
to connect to other chips.

00:51:25.920 --> 00:51:27.860
How long does it take to make a mask?

00:51:29.800 --> 00:51:34.000
What we see is time from tape-out
to first chips back.

00:51:34.720 --> 00:51:37.440
Again, depends on node,
but it's ballpark four or five months.

00:51:37.553 --> 00:51:40.764
So tape-out is just sending the file?

00:51:42.280 --> 00:51:45.080
We consider tape-out as sending the file,
and then there's a whole process of you

00:51:45.080 --> 00:51:48.731
make the masks for all the layers, and
then, actually just producing the chips.

00:51:49.080 --> 00:51:51.520
Producing the masks and producing
the chips happens after tape-out.

00:51:51.520 --> 00:51:52.480
That's right.
I see.

00:51:53.680 --> 00:51:56.880
Is the term tape-out from like you send
a magnetic tape with the

00:51:56.880 --> 00:51:58.560
instructions or something?
It could be.

00:51:58.560 --> 00:52:01.360
I was in software when the
software was created.

00:52:02.120 --> 00:52:04.000
I was curious about
the tape actually means.

00:52:04.000 --> 00:52:04.680
It feels like…

00:52:04.680 --> 00:52:09.440
When I think about AI predictions,
one thing I'm really struck by is how,

00:52:11.840 --> 00:52:15.280
still in 2026, every time you open a

00:52:16.320 --> 00:52:18.960
chat window, it's contextless.

00:52:19.600 --> 00:52:20.360
It's got no memory.

00:52:20.640 --> 00:52:22.880
Now, to be fair, it's like,
guys, it's been four years.

00:52:23.760 --> 00:52:25.920
Not even four years.
It's been three and a half years.

00:52:25.920 --> 00:52:27.440
Just calm down, we'll get there.

00:52:27.600 --> 00:52:32.440
But I also interpret
a lot of the current enthusiasm

00:52:32.440 --> 00:52:35.520
for OpenClaw and all that stuff as

00:52:36.360 --> 00:52:40.520
this super hacky backdoor into state

00:52:40.520 --> 00:52:45.320
management where your little claw will
write a markdown file of what it's doing

00:52:45.320 --> 00:52:48.320
and then look at that markdown file
the next time and things like that.

00:52:48.400 --> 00:52:53.080
But it just feels like
state management and memory is going to be

00:52:53.080 --> 00:52:57.600
a huge deal and that will really
change the character of AI products.

00:52:58.175 --> 00:52:59.460
It's really interesting.

00:53:01.600 --> 00:53:08.120
Long context is one of the biggest
bottlenecks on speed of the model.

00:53:09.800 --> 00:53:12.800
Every single token you generate,
it reads through all of the previous

00:53:12.800 --> 00:53:15.960
tokens, or maybe it reads through a subset
of them, but reads through a lot

00:53:15.960 --> 00:53:17.660
of the previous tokens you've written.

00:53:19.200 --> 00:53:21.448
Memory bandwidth for that is
really constraining.

00:53:21.532 --> 00:53:25.800
You can think of model-level ways to solve
that problem, which is to say

00:53:26.360 --> 00:53:29.000
maybe I can compress it into fewer
bytes or something like that.

00:53:29.480 --> 00:53:33.920
But it's interesting that the most
effective way to solve it has been—it's

00:53:33.920 --> 00:53:36.200
really a combination of everything—but
the most effective way to solve it has

00:53:36.200 --> 00:53:39.760
been
once you hit your 300,000 token limit,

00:53:40.400 --> 00:53:42.400
have the model go back
through it and compact.

00:53:44.800 --> 00:53:46.080
This is kind of what OpenClaw is doing.

00:53:46.080 --> 00:53:48.267
It's compacting everything you've done.

00:53:49.520 --> 00:53:52.187
But it's funny that it's so manual.

00:53:53.120 --> 00:53:55.400
I think— Manual is the wrong word.

00:53:55.720 --> 00:53:57.025
It's so primitive.

00:53:57.080 --> 00:53:58.720
It's maybe because it's so controllable.

00:54:00.520 --> 00:54:04.360
If you want to iterate on how you compact,
you give a different prompt, and you say,

00:54:04.360 --> 00:54:06.960
"Compact this way, compact that way." You
can iterate that on that

00:54:06.960 --> 00:54:08.200
in seconds or minutes.

00:54:08.840 --> 00:54:11.440
Whereas if you're trying to do some
iteration on the model level,

00:54:11.440 --> 00:54:14.000
where you say, "Now I've got a different
model architecture," it's going to take

00:54:14.000 --> 00:54:16.720
you months to train and launch something.
Yes.

00:54:17.080 --> 00:54:18.505
Any other AI predictions?

00:54:18.589 --> 00:54:22.920
I'm generally just interested in what
makes models cheaper and faster.

00:54:25.600 --> 00:54:28.760
Just at the model architecture level,
really tied into this context thing.

00:54:28.760 --> 00:54:32.000
I think the context size will stay
ballpark the same where it is,

00:54:32.000 --> 00:54:35.880
maybe a few times larger,
but the parameter count will go up.

00:54:36.040 --> 00:54:38.960
Parameter count should grow much faster
than context length actually,

00:54:38.960 --> 00:54:41.680
just because of the underlying
physics of what's available.

00:54:42.112 --> 00:54:45.520
Though, has that been the story?

00:54:46.080 --> 00:54:48.280
Would that be a reacceleration
of parameter count?

00:54:48.280 --> 00:54:52.560
Because it feels like we've leveled off
slightly in the last year or two,

00:54:52.560 --> 00:54:55.501
and instead we've been
focusing on more and better RL.

00:54:56.680 --> 00:54:59.560
Parameter count or
thinking tokens, I guess.

00:55:00.600 --> 00:55:06.011
Those are available, but the context
length is struggling to grow.

00:55:07.480 --> 00:55:08.200
But you think we…

00:55:08.600 --> 00:55:10.760
When you say context length is struggling
to grow, but you're saying we

00:55:10.760 --> 00:55:12.040
keep context the same length.

00:55:12.040 --> 00:55:13.200
Keep context the same length.

00:55:13.200 --> 00:55:15.040
But we're better at working
with large context.

00:55:15.040 --> 00:55:16.300
Is that what you're saying?

00:55:17.280 --> 00:55:22.400
Just have application-level interventions
to manage large context, like compacting.

00:55:22.800 --> 00:55:24.340
Because I think everyone's had
the experience

00:55:24.840 --> 00:55:28.784
currently of the chat conversation
and the further down in the chat you get.

00:55:28.867 --> 00:55:30.760
It just gets looser and—Yeah, sloppy.

00:55:30.760 --> 00:55:33.920
It's just like really sloppy by the end,
and it's like making mistakes, whatever.

00:55:33.920 --> 00:55:36.360
You're saying we start to do
better with large contacts.

00:55:36.880 --> 00:55:37.668
I buy that.

00:55:37.751 --> 00:55:43.520
When will I be typing into a chat window,
and it is a MatX chip

00:55:43.600 --> 00:55:45.133
underneath it, powering it?

00:55:45.720 --> 00:55:47.060
Tape-out in under a year.

00:55:47.480 --> 00:55:49.440
That means chips available end of year.
Ballpark.

00:55:52.000 --> 00:55:52.800
That's exciting.

00:55:52.800 --> 00:55:56.360
So in 2027, I will be seeing very high
performing chats as

00:55:56.360 --> 00:55:57.520
a result of your chips.

00:55:57.520 --> 00:56:00.065
In the 1% experiment of the users
or something like that.

00:56:00.148 --> 00:56:00.880
Exactly.

00:56:00.880 --> 00:56:04.080
I need to find a way to finagle
myself into the A/B test.

00:56:04.080 --> 00:56:08.480
MatX is 100 people?
That's right.

00:56:09.440 --> 00:56:13.880
How have you gone about
building the team, the culture?

00:56:16.560 --> 00:56:20.640
What we have on the team is hardware,
mostly hardware, but a big software

00:56:20.640 --> 00:56:22.200
team and also a big ML team.

00:56:23.480 --> 00:56:27.240
I think the ML team is quite
unusual in what we ask them to do.

00:56:27.560 --> 00:56:32.920
When you look at a typical ML team
in an AI chip company, it will be

00:56:33.960 --> 00:56:36.640
what I might say ML
engineering or ML performance.

00:56:37.360 --> 00:56:43.000
They're writing kernels that actually
just use your hardware

00:56:43.000 --> 00:56:44.280
as well on a given model.

00:56:44.280 --> 00:56:45.760
There's a missed opportunity there.

00:56:45.760 --> 00:56:48.440
If you're saying all we do is we take
other people's models,

00:56:48.440 --> 00:56:52.000
and we write kernels for them,
you're optimizing this, but you

00:56:52.000 --> 00:56:53.480
can't optimize this at the same time.

00:56:53.480 --> 00:56:57.400
We want to optimize the whole thing
at the same time, real code design.

00:56:57.497 --> 00:57:00.080
Our ML team is actual real ML research.

00:57:00.720 --> 00:57:07.440
What they do every day is they train
small LLMs from scratch focusing

00:57:07.440 --> 00:57:10.280
on numerics and attention.

00:57:12.120 --> 00:57:17.400
This has really, really helped us
make an interesting product.

00:57:18.120 --> 00:57:20.520
It's shown up most
strongly in our numerics.

00:57:23.640 --> 00:57:26.280
Often what you see when people design
numerics is they say,

00:57:27.560 --> 00:57:30.920
back when Float32 was popular,
it would be, "I'm going to follow the IEEE

00:57:30.920 --> 00:57:34.320
standard." Now it is like,
follow the Open Compute standard.

00:57:34.320 --> 00:57:38.163
There are lots of little details where you
say things like

00:57:38.960 --> 00:57:41.300
maybe, "What's the rounding mode I'm going
to use?" Like,

00:57:41.440 --> 00:57:44.020
"Round to the nearest even," or something
like that, which is like

00:57:45.040 --> 00:57:46.720
the best known standard for how to round.

00:57:46.880 --> 00:57:48.640
We want to cut corners anywhere we can.

00:57:49.920 --> 00:57:54.080
Maybe don't do the best rounding, maybe
don't do the…

00:57:54.160 --> 00:57:56.020
Don't get all the corner cases correctly.

00:57:56.080 --> 00:57:59.280
That's a very scary proposition if you're
just making those choices blind,

00:57:59.280 --> 00:58:03.480
but if you have the benefit of a research
team who can back you up as you do that,

00:58:05.160 --> 00:58:07.900
it's really powerful,
and it's really interesting that we can

00:58:08.440 --> 00:58:10.862
make some sloppy choices in these cases.

00:58:11.880 --> 00:58:17.000
I feel like often technical advances
come through better iteration loops.

00:58:17.320 --> 00:58:20.960
A favorite example of this I found
recently was that the Wright brothers

00:58:20.960 --> 00:58:24.680
actually had a failed
season before first flight.

00:58:25.040 --> 00:58:29.040
First flight was in 1904,
and they were down in Kitty Hawk in 1903

00:58:29.200 --> 00:58:30.720
and not making that much progress.

00:58:31.000 --> 00:58:34.720
They went back
to Ohio, and they had a wind tunnel,

00:58:34.880 --> 00:58:37.160
and they were testing their
design in the wind tunnel.

00:58:37.160 --> 00:58:41.040
You can imagine not a lot
of wind tunnels in 1904.

00:58:42.200 --> 00:58:46.565
They did a lot of wind tunnel testing and
their successful flight was after that.

00:58:46.648 --> 00:58:50.080
Is this something you're focused on where,
to get better chips,

00:58:50.080 --> 00:58:53.600
you allow for a better
testing and iteration loop,

00:58:53.600 --> 00:58:55.320
and what does that look like?

00:58:56.440 --> 00:59:01.160
I think this mostly happens in the
architecture and product definition stage.

00:59:02.200 --> 00:59:06.120
Maybe even more generally, I think
AI chips seem to live or die

00:59:06.120 --> 00:59:07.720
by product definition and architecture.

00:59:08.545 --> 00:59:11.480
What is the most extreme form of fast
iteration is doing it in your head.

00:59:12.840 --> 00:59:15.040
Can you map a model
to hardware in your head?

00:59:15.040 --> 00:59:17.720
Can you estimate the performance
of what it is in your head?

00:59:18.840 --> 00:59:21.120
You're not going to be 100% perfect,
but maybe you can prove some

00:59:21.120 --> 00:59:22.460
lower bound in performance.

00:59:24.640 --> 00:59:30.520
The simplest possible thing is my model
has a trillion parameters, my device

00:59:31.640 --> 00:59:34.440
can do a billion multipliers per second,
so it takes 1,000 seconds to run or

00:59:34.440 --> 00:59:36.940
something like that,
just to do that simple division.

00:59:37.000 --> 00:59:39.960
But then there are much more complicated
things like we tend to look at

00:59:40.920 --> 00:59:43.560
resource balances,
like how many memory fetches do I need

00:59:43.560 --> 00:59:45.680
to do per a multiplier
or something like that.

00:59:49.320 --> 00:59:54.720
At least the way I like to do design
and architecture and optimization is to

00:59:55.280 --> 00:59:58.560
be able to estimate the performance
to within about 30-40%

00:59:59.840 --> 01:00:01.840
before even typing anything in at all.

01:00:02.640 --> 01:00:04.100
We've tried to do that a lot.

01:00:05.680 --> 01:00:07.780
A lot of our architecture
comes from there.

01:00:08.960 --> 01:00:10.960
Then the next stage of iteration is…

01:00:11.800 --> 01:00:13.000
That's on the performance side.

01:00:13.000 --> 01:00:16.120
This also happens
on the circuit design side as well.

01:00:16.280 --> 01:00:19.000
Can you take a circuit and say
what is the gate count on that?

01:00:20.992 --> 01:00:24.440
A 16-bit multiplier has approximately 16
squared mini-gates,

01:00:24.760 --> 01:00:28.680
and you can do that for more complicated
things by sorting networks and so on.

01:00:29.640 --> 01:00:33.040
We already have a pretty good idea
of the costs and speeds of things at that

01:00:33.040 --> 01:00:34.940
point after doing these calculations.

01:00:36.200 --> 01:00:39.560
Then what we tend to do as
the next step of iteration is

01:00:40.160 --> 01:00:42.320
on the ML side, we run model experiments.

01:00:42.320 --> 01:00:44.880
You get iteration speed just
by having small models mostly.

01:00:48.880 --> 01:00:53.080
On the hardware side,
we use performance simulators to do

01:00:53.080 --> 01:00:55.360
the next level of detail to make sure
we're seeing all

01:00:55.360 --> 01:00:56.660
the things we want to see.

01:00:56.800 --> 01:01:01.720
This idea that the best iteration is
in your head is reminding

01:01:01.720 --> 01:01:03.076
me of Jeff Dean's numbers...

01:01:04.160 --> 01:01:06.640
Do you have your equivalent
of that numbers every MatX?

01:01:06.960 --> 01:01:11.320
We have go/gates in our company,
which says, what is the cost of an XOR

01:01:11.320 --> 01:01:15.672
gate, an AND gate, a full adder,
SRAM bitcell, and so on.

01:01:15.960 --> 01:01:17.880
You want people to be working
with that stuff in their head and have

01:01:17.880 --> 01:01:22.280
an intuitive sense for it because
it leads to better iteration.

01:01:22.360 --> 01:01:25.599
What is the pitch to someone joining MatX?

01:01:26.182 --> 01:01:30.200
I think if you are someone who likes
optimizing,

01:01:30.440 --> 01:01:36.120
just optimize something—software,
hardware, factorial,

01:01:36.120 --> 01:01:41.200
whatever—if you're trying to fit something
into the smallest budget possible, I

01:01:41.200 --> 01:01:43.060
think it's a pretty exciting place to be.

01:01:44.960 --> 01:01:50.080
I think hardware companies in general are
really exciting because you have such a

01:01:50.640 --> 01:01:52.760
broad range of skills
of people on the team.

01:01:52.760 --> 01:01:54.120
You have software people,
you have hardware people,

01:01:54.120 --> 01:01:57.480
you've got physical design,
you've got people who are just looking at

01:01:57.480 --> 01:02:00.640
the insertion force of a card into a rack.

01:02:02.000 --> 01:02:05.040
So there's so much discussion
and learning you can do.

01:02:06.000 --> 01:02:11.800
I think MatX in particular,
we really care about this and I think we

01:02:11.800 --> 01:02:13.600
extended it all the way up
into the application

01:02:13.600 --> 01:02:14.920
and the machine learning as well.

01:02:20.480 --> 01:02:23.680
Really, really interesting technical
problems, and I think just generally

01:02:25.360 --> 01:02:26.920
there's lots of interesting
people to talk to.

01:02:26.920 --> 01:02:27.420
Yes.

01:02:27.480 --> 01:02:32.120
Presumably in terms of impact,
if you can design a meaningfully higher

01:02:32.120 --> 01:02:37.400
throughput chip, a 20% higher throughput
chip means 20% more AI is happening.

01:02:37.560 --> 01:02:41.320
If the bottleneck is elsewhere, like
power or something like that or cost,

01:02:41.800 --> 01:02:44.600
you actually just are
meaningfully increasing the amount

01:02:44.600 --> 01:02:48.098
of intelligence in the world,
which is presumably exciting to people.

01:02:49.000 --> 01:02:54.640
I think this shows up both as just
can it apply in more applications,

01:02:54.640 --> 01:02:57.565
as well as just how smart is the model.
Yes.

01:02:57.840 --> 01:02:58.880
How about Rust?

01:02:59.526 --> 01:03:03.360
A previous project I worked on at Google,
we did a lot of Haskell.

01:03:04.320 --> 01:03:08.560
I did Haskell when I was at school.
I loved it.

01:03:08.880 --> 01:03:10.800
Very principled, very interesting.

01:03:10.800 --> 01:03:12.960
I like Haskell, but I also
like making stuff fast.

01:03:14.120 --> 01:03:17.440
The question is,
what is the first thing you want to do?

01:03:17.440 --> 01:03:19.320
You want to be able to modify your memory.

01:03:19.320 --> 01:03:21.160
Haskell, you jump
through hoops to do that.

01:03:21.720 --> 01:03:24.720
Maybe I just want a language that is
functional programming

01:03:24.720 --> 01:03:26.380
that lets me modify my memory.

01:03:26.680 --> 01:03:31.599
I think Rust has a lot of the nice things
which are like type classes or traits

01:03:32.840 --> 01:03:34.520
and a rich type system.

01:03:35.480 --> 01:03:40.400
One of the things that we have done,
interesting ways we use it at MatX are

01:03:41.160 --> 01:03:44.840
the range of data types
that you express on software.

01:03:45.240 --> 01:03:46.320
What are the integer types?

01:03:46.560 --> 01:03:49.920
Int32, int64, int8,
maybe that's all you care about.

01:03:50.560 --> 01:03:55.040
But it turns out in hardware,
you care about every single bit, and so

01:03:55.280 --> 01:03:58.160
you want to use 17, 18, 19-bit integers.

01:04:01.120 --> 01:04:06.134
That is quite natural to express,
and we build up a whole ecosystem of

01:04:07.760 --> 01:04:10.600
rich hardware data types in Rust as well.

01:04:11.181 --> 01:04:17.240
Has Rust beaten Go for the position
of performant type programming language

01:04:17.240 --> 01:04:19.640
with modern features or do they
actually address different?

01:04:22.320 --> 01:04:28.560
There's what the Rust marketing will say,
which is "safe without a garbage

01:04:28.560 --> 01:04:31.640
collection," which I think is a real…

01:04:31.640 --> 01:04:37.040
It is the objective thing that you can say
is different, but sort of buries the lead,

01:04:37.040 --> 01:04:41.628
which is also just it's got nice type
system features that Go doesn't have.

01:04:42.720 --> 01:04:44.040
Why is garbage collection…

01:04:44.040 --> 01:04:45.200
Why does it matter at all?

01:04:46.680 --> 01:04:49.400
People often focus on the time it takes
to run a garbage collector,

01:04:49.400 --> 01:04:52.920
but the other thing is that every time you
allocate an object, you've got the object,

01:04:52.920 --> 01:04:55.600
and then you've got the garbage
collector header at the beginning.

01:04:55.600 --> 01:04:57.400
It uses a lot more memory as well.

01:04:57.400 --> 01:05:01.320
If you want to design some,
I don't know, data structure that uses

01:05:01.320 --> 01:05:04.275
the right amount of memory
rather than a bit more than…

01:05:04.800 --> 01:05:07.560
I hadn't realized that in Rust you're
allocating your memory manually

01:05:07.560 --> 01:05:09.680
versus in Go you have— That's right.

01:05:10.782 --> 01:05:12.826
I didn't realize that.
And you prefer that for what you're doing.

01:05:13.200 --> 01:05:15.400
I just really like
dealing with the details.

01:05:15.400 --> 01:05:16.760
Like, you give me a puzzle,
and I'll be like,

01:05:16.760 --> 01:05:20.320
"Let me solve every single piece of it."
That tickles that part

01:05:20.320 --> 01:05:21.520
of my mind with Rust.

01:05:21.520 --> 01:05:24.160
It seems like you're a fan
of optimization generally.

01:05:24.160 --> 01:05:26.120
Is that a fair characterization?
Yeah.

01:05:26.120 --> 01:05:27.000
Where else have you…

01:05:27.240 --> 01:05:31.010
Chip optimization is one
domain, where else?

01:05:36.120 --> 01:05:39.240
When one of the really exciting things I
found about working at Google is

01:05:39.400 --> 01:05:42.520
that the whole Google code base is
available, and you can look at

01:05:43.080 --> 01:05:46.600
how does a memory allocator work,
how does a mutex work,

01:05:46.600 --> 01:05:49.640
how does a HashMap work,
any of those things, and you can go

01:05:49.640 --> 01:05:51.400
and look inside the implementations.

01:05:51.400 --> 01:05:56.440
Google has excellent implementations of
those, some of the best you could write.

01:05:59.320 --> 01:06:02.920
One of the things I did on my nights
and weekends when I was at Google was just

01:06:02.920 --> 01:06:07.080
to go find those implementations,
write a benchmark.

01:06:07.080 --> 01:06:10.280
How many nanoseconds does it take
to allocate eight bytes of memory?

01:06:11.520 --> 01:06:12.520
Can I make that faster?

01:06:13.960 --> 01:06:15.480
Can I maybe I inline this function?

01:06:15.560 --> 01:06:18.120
Maybe I look at the assembly and say,
"It looks like

01:06:18.840 --> 01:06:21.720
there's a few memory moves here or
there are some registers that are being

01:06:21.720 --> 01:06:24.920
used that I don't need in the
fast path and in the slow path.

01:06:25.080 --> 01:06:28.680
Can I do something there?"
I don't know, that was always my

01:06:30.360 --> 01:06:32.030
just fun and learning activity.

01:06:32.113 --> 01:06:36.640
Being outside of Google, I feel,
I probably could have done this inside

01:06:36.640 --> 01:06:41.640
of Google as well, but outside of Google,
I felt the luxury to be able to talk about

01:06:42.280 --> 01:06:43.400
these results as well.

01:06:43.875 --> 01:06:49.880
One of the things I've looked at recently
is just hash tables are used so much.

01:06:51.880 --> 01:06:57.120
One prompt for me was,
if I wanted to design custom CPU

01:06:57.120 --> 01:06:58.640
instructions for accelerating hash tables…

01:06:58.640 --> 01:07:00.120
Hash tables are one
of the most common things.

01:07:00.120 --> 01:07:02.040
I'm looking at them up
and writing them all the time.

01:07:02.040 --> 01:07:04.200
What would the optimal CPU be for that?

01:07:07.523 --> 01:07:12.480
Following down that chain is like,
what is the best hash table

01:07:12.480 --> 01:07:14.140
implementation in the first place?

01:07:14.360 --> 01:07:18.240
I spent some time looking at
different SIMD implementations.

01:07:18.440 --> 01:07:21.440
There's this really cool technique called
'cuckoo hashing' where you

01:07:22.038 --> 01:07:26.800
hash into two different locations,
and then you use the bucket

01:07:26.800 --> 01:07:27.600
which is less full.

01:07:30.160 --> 01:07:35.040
It's been in the literature for decades
and yet the best hash table

01:07:35.040 --> 01:07:38.320
implementations don't use it
because it's somehow not practical.

01:07:39.640 --> 01:07:40.560
Why is it not practical?

01:07:40.682 --> 01:07:45.040
Practical hash tables are
these days considered to be ones that use

01:07:46.560 --> 01:07:51.040
SIMD vector instructions to scan
eight buckets at a time.

01:07:54.800 --> 01:07:57.720
The way cuckoo hashing is normally
described is I look up one

01:07:57.720 --> 01:07:59.325
bucket here and one bucket there.

01:07:59.409 --> 01:08:00.960
I'm not using the vector instructions.

01:08:00.960 --> 01:08:03.720
Vector instructions are much faster than
scalar instructions and so

01:08:03.720 --> 01:08:05.120
there's a missed opportunity.

01:08:05.760 --> 01:08:08.520
Again, just
take the two good ideas and stick them

01:08:08.520 --> 01:08:10.795
together, do vector
instructions on cuckoo hashing.

01:08:13.120 --> 01:08:15.360
You have to be careful to get the details
right, but if you get it right,

01:08:15.360 --> 01:08:16.680
you can actually just wing it.

01:08:16.884 --> 01:08:22.280
Sorry, is your claim that one could design
a custom CPU that has way better hash

01:08:22.280 --> 01:08:26.280
table performance,
or even on current chips, you could

01:08:26.280 --> 01:08:28.020
get way better hash table performance?

01:08:28.560 --> 01:08:29.060
Both.

01:08:31.280 --> 01:08:33.720
I'm interested in what you can design
in custom hardware,

01:08:33.720 --> 01:08:35.080
but MatX doesn't make CPUs.

01:08:35.080 --> 01:08:36.960
We're not going to make CPUs.
You could.

01:08:36.960 --> 01:08:38.323
New line of business.

01:08:39.840 --> 01:08:43.411
We just want to focus on shipping
one product well for the time being.

01:08:43.840 --> 01:08:44.640
Fair.
Good answer.

01:08:45.204 --> 01:08:49.680
I think it's an interesting exercise,
but I don't get to feel the endorphins

01:08:49.680 --> 01:08:51.360
of seeing the number go down.

01:08:52.040 --> 01:08:54.720
I first did this on just Intel CPUs.

01:08:55.520 --> 01:08:59.480
You can get better performance
than some of the best hash table

01:08:59.480 --> 01:09:03.473
implementations available
using cuckoo hashing on Intel CPUs.

01:09:05.080 --> 01:09:11.120
What are examples of workloads that are
really hash table read intensive?

01:09:11.600 --> 01:09:15.040
I know everything,
JavaScript, I guess, but…

01:09:18.488 --> 01:09:22.520
It's a tricky exercise because when you
really think about it, you're like,

01:09:22.520 --> 01:09:23.840
"Did I really need a hash table there?

01:09:23.840 --> 01:09:26.560
I probably didn't, but I just reach for it
all the time." But you can go

01:09:26.560 --> 01:09:30.520
to the Google JavaScript team and probably
help them eke out better performance

01:09:30.520 --> 01:09:32.800
in the Chrome JavaScript engine?
Potentially.

01:09:33.760 --> 01:09:35.200
I'm not going to spend my time on that.

01:09:35.200 --> 01:09:38.720
If they're listening to this podcast,
just a free idea from Reiner.

01:09:39.120 --> 01:09:40.400
Explain the dragon.

01:09:41.480 --> 01:09:44.440
This is from a book that,
when I was working on the JAX team…

01:09:44.600 --> 01:09:47.720
The JAX team is one of the ML
infrastructure teams at Google.

01:09:47.720 --> 01:09:50.000
I was there as the most
recent team before I left.

01:09:50.720 --> 01:09:52.360
I'm sorry, what does the JAX team do?

01:09:52.960 --> 01:09:54.399
The JAX team develops…

01:09:54.982 --> 01:10:00.040
This is Google's new, more modern version
of TensorFlow or a competitor to PyTorch.

01:10:00.280 --> 01:10:03.408
It's how you write models
in Python to run on TPUs.

01:10:03.491 --> 01:10:07.560
A big part of the JAX team, however, is to
say, we have JAX, the technical artifact.

01:10:08.440 --> 01:10:13.626
Can we help enable users to actually use
it really well and get high performance?

01:10:14.920 --> 01:10:17.040
Ultimately that became, who are the users?

01:10:17.040 --> 01:10:18.200
It's people writing LLMs.

01:10:18.200 --> 01:10:20.220
How do you get good performance on LLMs?

01:10:20.842 --> 01:10:24.600
Really, really strong team,
the JAX team at Google,

01:10:24.600 --> 01:10:27.400
although as with a lot of brain
people are now elsewhere as well.

01:10:28.040 --> 01:10:31.320
We developed a lot of the different
techniques for how to lay out

01:10:31.320 --> 01:10:33.040
models efficiently on many chips.

01:10:34.280 --> 01:10:38.440
Ultimately, some people at Google—and I
contributed after I left Google—wrote this

01:10:39.320 --> 01:10:43.240
guide called How&lt;i&gt; To Scale Your Model,&lt;/i&gt;
how to run an LLM as fast as possible.

01:10:44.040 --> 01:10:47.840
It is the main reference for how
to get high performance on TPUs.

01:10:47.840 --> 01:10:49.960
There is now also a GPU
version of this as well.

01:10:49.960 --> 01:10:52.040
It's a dragon because it's
How&lt;i&gt; to Train Your Dragon.&lt;/i&gt;

01:10:52.920 --> 01:10:53.420
I see.

01:10:55.560 --> 01:10:56.200
Last question.

01:10:57.800 --> 01:11:02.520
People might not have thought that
there's room for new chip companies.

01:11:02.520 --> 01:11:04.440
It might have seemed unusual or very hard.

01:11:05.520 --> 01:11:08.840
You guys, it seems like a very
good approach with that.

01:11:09.080 --> 01:11:14.120
Where do you think are other opportunities
for companies to be started here in 2026?

01:11:14.520 --> 01:11:17.640
Where do you think people should be
looking for entrepreneurial opportunities

01:11:17.640 --> 01:11:21.194
or just technical challenges
that haven't been properly addressed?

01:11:21.560 --> 01:11:23.400
More labs, I think, is still interesting.

01:11:24.720 --> 01:11:27.640
Just can we do more on model
architecture is always interesting.

01:11:27.640 --> 01:11:30.328
You think we have not fully
explored model architecture space?

01:11:30.760 --> 01:11:33.900
The Frontier Labs have done a pretty good
job of exploring it, but I think,

01:11:36.840 --> 01:11:40.421
as the hardware changes, the shape
of the model should change for sure.

01:11:41.360 --> 01:11:43.920
Presumably you're not thinking like,
yet another Frontier Lab

01:11:44.280 --> 01:11:45.720
pursuing the same architecture.

01:11:45.720 --> 01:11:49.280
You think there's probably off the wall
looking architectures that will actually

01:11:49.280 --> 01:11:53.392
make a lot of— I think there's
a little bit off the wall for sure.

01:11:53.760 --> 01:11:55.760
Do you have a specific
architecture in mind?

01:11:55.760 --> 01:11:58.440
My mentality is always sticking within
the transformer family, but

01:12:00.040 --> 01:12:01.940
what are the constraints that are
currently available,

01:12:02.040 --> 01:12:04.600
currently imposed that you could lift?

01:12:05.920 --> 01:12:09.920
For example, one of the things is there's
this idea, when you're doing transformer

01:12:10.120 --> 01:12:13.600
inference, you do
pre-fill that is processing what the user

01:12:13.600 --> 01:12:16.840
said to you, and then there's decode
which is generating the response to that.

01:12:17.250 --> 01:12:22.040
Those are totally different in pretty much
every aspect of how they actually run.

01:12:22.360 --> 01:12:25.080
One runs a step at a time,
the other one runs really in parallel.

01:12:26.120 --> 01:12:29.440
There is this somewhat artificial
constraint today that those are

01:12:29.440 --> 01:12:31.100
the same model that's doing both.

01:12:31.480 --> 01:12:32.680
Maybe lift that constraint.

01:12:33.560 --> 01:12:36.520
Another example would be,
there's this idea that the model that you…

01:12:37.800 --> 01:12:40.320
This is more fundamental constraint
that you have to train

01:12:40.320 --> 01:12:41.860
the same model as you serve.

01:12:42.720 --> 01:12:44.880
But again, training is very
different from serving.

01:12:45.440 --> 01:12:47.320
At training, it's very compute intensive.

01:12:47.320 --> 01:12:49.440
At serving, it's more
memory bandwidth intensive.

01:12:50.200 --> 01:12:54.880
Maybe, is there a way you can make a model
that when you use it at inference time, it

01:12:56.000 --> 01:12:59.360
increases the amount of computing it does
to use some of the available resources?

01:12:59.880 --> 01:13:01.680
Makes sense.
Reiner, thank you.

01:13:01.840 --> 01:13:02.400
Pleasure.

